// Rep Max Learn lessons: one per topic. Fields: s, t, kind (method, idea or writing), why, hook (idea topics only), chunks, example (task, steps, answer), selfx, faded, trap, recap. Written by Sonnet, checked by Sonnet, flagged parts settled by Opus.
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "This underpins every Paper 1 and 2 answer: you will use ceteris paribus and assumptions in diagrams, and evaluate models in short and extended questions.", "hook": {"q": "A shop cuts its prices and sales fall in the same month. What should you check first before deciding that cutting prices lowers sales?", "o": ["Whether anything else changed that could affect sales", "Nothing; the fall proves the effect", "Whether the shop is large", "Whether the prices were exactly 10% lower"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: other changes, such as a rival or a holiday, may explain the fall.", "Wrong: sales falling alongside a price cut does not show the price cut caused it.", "Wrong: size does not tell you whether another factor changed.", "Wrong: the exact size does not address other factors affecting sales."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Economics as social science", "b": "Economics studies how people, firms and governments make choices. It uses scientific methods: build a **model**, make predictions, then test them against evidence. It is a **social science** because it studies human behaviour, which is less predictable than natural matter."}, {"h": "Models and assumptions", "b": "A **model** is a simplified version of reality. It uses **assumptions**, such as rational consumers or perfect information, to make analysis manageable. Simplifying brings clarity. The cost is that unrealistic assumptions can make predictions less accurate."}, {"h": "Ceteris paribus", "b": "**Ceteris paribus** means 'all other things being equal'. You change one variable and hold others constant, so you can see its effect alone. A demand curve shifts only when a factor other than price changes. It is an assumption, not a claim about reality."}, {"h": "No controlled experiments", "b": "Natural scientists can run **controlled experiments**. Economists usually cannot, because an economy cannot be put in a laboratory and many variables change together. So economists rely on models, data and **ceteris paribus** to isolate effects."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why economists use ceteris paribus when analysing the effect of a fall in the price of cinema tickets on the quantity demanded.", "steps": [{"do": "State the problem: the quantity of cinema tickets demanded is affected by price, income, tastes and the price of substitutes.", "why": "Shows that many variables influence the outcome, so one change is hard to isolate."}, {"do": "Apply ceteris paribus: assume income, tastes and the price of streaming stay constant while ticket prices fall.", "why": "Holding other factors constant isolates the effect of price alone."}, {"do": "Link to the model: a fall in price causes an extension along the demand curve, so quantity demanded rises.", "why": "Uses correct terminology: movement along, not a shift of, the curve."}, {"do": "Explain why this is needed: economists cannot run a controlled experiment on the whole economy to hold factors constant.", "why": "Connects ceteris paribus to the limits of a social science."}, {"do": "Note the limit: in reality other factors change too, so actual quantity demanded may differ from the prediction.", "why": "Brief evaluation of the assumption."}], "answer": "Many factors affect demand for cinema tickets. Ceteris paribus holds all but price constant, so a price fall causes an extension along demand and quantity rises. It is needed as economists cannot run controlled experiments, though real outcomes may differ."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say the effect is an extension along the demand curve rather than a shift?", "o": ["Only price changed, and other factors were held constant", "Income rose at the same time", "Demand curves never shift", "Ceteris paribus means nothing changes"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a price change alone moves you along the curve; other factors changing would shift it.", "Wrong: if income rose, the curve would shift; the example held income constant.", "Wrong: demand curves shift when non-price factors change.", "Wrong: ceteris paribus holds other variables constant, while the studied variable still changes."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why an economist's prediction that higher interest rates reduce consumer spending might not match real data.", "given": ["Step 1: The model predicts that higher interest rates reduce spending, ceteris paribus.", "Step 2: This assumes income, confidence and prices are unchanged."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["Note that in reality other factors may change at the same time, so the prediction may not hold", "Conclude that the model is proved wrong", "Say economists can run controlled experiments to check", "State that ceteris paribus guarantees accuracy"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: real data mix many changes, so a gap does not mean the model is wrong.", "Wrong: a mismatch may be due to other factors changing, so it does not prove the model wrong.", "Wrong: controlled experiments on whole economies are generally impossible.", "Wrong: ceteris paribus is an assumption, not a guarantee of accuracy."]}, "trap": "Don't treat ceteris paribus as a description of reality. It is an assumption to isolate one effect; real data often reflect several changes at once.", "recap": "Economists use simplified models and ceteris paribus to isolate effects, because they cannot run controlled experiments on whole economies."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Questions on this topic appear as short multiple-choice or data-response items, and the skill underpins evaluation in every essay where policy judgements are made.", "hook": {"q": "Which claim could you settle by looking at data?", "o": ["Council tax is unfair", "Council tax should be abolished", "Council tax raised more last year than the year before", "Council tax is the worst tax"], "a": 2, "f": ["'Unfair' is an opinion about fairness, so data cannot settle it.", "'Should' is a value judgement, so data alone cannot settle it.", "Yes. Revenue figures can be checked, so the claim is testable.", "'Worst' is an opinion, so data cannot settle it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Positive statements", "b": "A **positive statement** is about what is, was or will be. It can be tested against evidence, so it can be shown **true or false**. A wrong claim is still positive if evidence could test it."}, {"h": "Normative statements", "b": "A **normative statement** contains a **value judgement** about what ought to be. Words like should, fair, too much and better are clues. Data cannot prove or disprove it, though data can inform the debate."}, {"h": "Value judgements in policy", "b": "Economists can use positive analysis to show a policy's effects. Choosing between outcomes, such as **equity** or **efficiency**, needs value judgements. Different people weigh outcomes differently, so they may disagree even with the same evidence."}, {"h": "Mixed statements", "b": "Many sentences combine both types. Split them into parts. 'Inflation rose, which is bad' has a testable claim and a **value judgement**. Do not label a sentence positive just because it has a number in it."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why 'Raising the tobacco tax will cut smoking, so the government should raise it' contains both positive and normative elements.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the first part: 'raising the tobacco tax will cut smoking'.", "why": "It predicts a cause and effect that sales data could test, so it is positive."}, {"do": "Note that it could be false if demand is very inelastic.", "why": "Positive statements can be wrong; being testable is what matters."}, {"do": "Identify the second part: 'the government should raise it'.", "why": "'Should' expresses a value judgement about what ought to happen, so it is normative."}, {"do": "Explain that evidence alone cannot justify 'should'.", "why": "The conclusion also depends on how one values health against, say, personal choice or the burden on poorer smokers."}], "answer": "The first part is positive because sales data could test whether the tax cuts smoking. The second is normative because 'should' reflects a value judgement. Evidence informs the policy, but the choice depends on values."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the first part of the example still positive even if it might be false?", "o": ["Because it contains the word 'will'", "Because it can be tested against evidence", "Because the government believes it", "Because it is about a tax"], "a": 1, "f": ["'Will' is not the test; a prediction is positive because evidence can check it.", "Correct. Positive means testable, whether the result turns out true or false.", "Government belief is opinion; it does not decide whether a claim is testable.", "Subject matter does not decide it; testability does."]}, "faded": {"task": "Classify: 'Cutting corporation tax will raise investment, which is the most important aim for the UK.'", "given": ["Split the sentence into two parts.", "Part one: 'cutting corporation tax will raise investment'."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Classify part one as normative because it is about policy", "Classify part one as positive, since investment data could test it", "Classify the whole sentence as positive because it is about investment", "Skip part one as it cannot be classified"], "a": 1, "f": ["Policy topics are not automatically normative; the claim is about cause and effect.", "Yes. Investment data could test the claim, so it is positive. Then classify 'most important aim' as normative.", "The second part is a value judgement, so the whole sentence is not positive.", "It can be classified: it is testable, so positive."]}, "trap": "Do not call a statement positive just because it has a number or sounds factual, or normative because it might be false. Ask: can evidence test it, or does it rely on opinion?", "recap": "Positive statements can be tested against evidence, true or false; normative statements rest on value judgements about what ought to be."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Scarcity and opportunity cost underpin every Edexcel economics paper; you use them to define terms and to explain choices made by consumers, firms and governments.", "hook": {"q": "You have £10 and can buy a cinema ticket, a meal or a book. You pick the meal. What have you really given up?", "o": ["The cinema ticket and the book together", "Only the next best of the other two", "The £10 itself", "Nothing, since you got the meal"], "a": 1, "f": ["You can only do one alternative instead, so you give up the best other option, not both.", "Right: the cost of a choice is the best alternative you gave up.", "The £10 is the money spent; the economic cost is the benefit of the best alternative use.", "Choosing always means giving something up, because resources are limited."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "The basic problem", "b": "**Scarcity** means finite resources but unlimited wants. It is the **economic problem**. Because of it, every economy must decide what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. It affects rich and poor countries alike."}, {"h": "Types of resource", "b": "**Renewable resources** can regenerate, such as fish stocks or forests, but only if not overused. **Non-renewable resources**, such as oil and coal, are finite and cannot be replaced on a human timescale. Both are scarce."}, {"h": "Opportunity cost", "b": "**Opportunity cost** is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. It is one option, not the total of all alternatives. It applies to consumers, firms and governments, and need not be a money amount."}, {"h": "Choice links them", "b": "Scarcity forces **choice**, and choice creates opportunity cost. If resources were unlimited, nothing would need to be given up. This chain of reasoning is how you build an explanation in the exam."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a government that builds a new school has an opportunity cost.", "steps": [{"do": "State that resources are scarce: the government has limited funds, land and labour.", "why": "Scarcity is the starting point of the economic problem."}, {"do": "State that wants for public spending are unlimited, such as health, defence and transport.", "why": "Scarce resources against unlimited wants makes choice necessary."}, {"do": "Say that using resources on the school means they cannot be used elsewhere.", "why": "Resources have alternative uses, so choosing one excludes another."}, {"do": "Identify the next best use, for example a new hospital ward.", "why": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative, so name it."}, {"do": "Conclude that the benefit of that ward is the opportunity cost of the school.", "why": "This links the choice to the definition."}], "answer": "Funds are scarce but wants for spending are unlimited, so the government must choose. Resources used on a school cannot be used elsewhere. The benefit of the next best alternative, such as a hospital ward, is the opportunity cost."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer name only one alternative, the hospital ward, as the opportunity cost?", "o": ["Opportunity cost is the next best alternative only", "Governments can only ever choose between two options", "Other alternatives have no value to the economy", "Opportunity cost must be measured in money terms"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: opportunity cost is the value of the single next best alternative forgone.", "Governments face many options; the definition, not the number of options, limits the cost to one.", "Other options may be valuable; they are simply not the next best.", "Opportunity cost need not be in money; it can be benefits forgone."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a firm using its factory to make chairs has an opportunity cost.", "given": ["Resources such as machines, labour and floor space are scarce.", "Using them for chairs means they cannot be used to make tables."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Say the opportunity cost is the cost of the chairs' materials", "Say the opportunity cost is the profit from the next best product, such as tables", "Say that the firm has no opportunity cost because it makes a profit", "Say the opportunity cost is all other products the firm could make"], "a": 1, "f": ["Material cost is a money cost of production; opportunity cost is the value of the alternative forgone.", "Right: identify the next best alternative and link its value to the definition.", "Profit does not remove opportunity cost; the best alternative use was still given up.", "Only the next best alternative counts, not all products combined."]}, "trap": "Do not list every forgone option or quote the money price. Name the single next best alternative given up, and say it is its value that is the cost.", "recap": "Scarcity forces choice, and the opportunity cost of any choice is the value of the next best alternative given up."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "PPF diagrams appear in short-answer and data questions, and support analysis of growth, efficiency and opportunity cost in essays throughout Theme 1 and Theme 3.", "hook": {"q": "A town has a fixed number of workers and can build either houses or roads. If it builds more houses, what happens to the number of roads it can build?", "o": ["It must build fewer roads", "It can build more roads too", "It builds the same number of roads", "It can build unlimited roads"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: resources are scarce, so using more on houses leaves fewer for roads. That is opportunity cost.", "Only possible if there were unused resources or growth, not with fixed resources fully used.", "Resources are limited, so extra houses must take resources away from roads.", "Scarcity means output cannot be unlimited."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What the PPF shows", "b": "A **production possibility frontier** shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources and technology. Points on it are **productively efficient**. Points inside show unemployed or misallocated resources. Points outside are **unattainable**."}, {"h": "Opportunity cost", "b": "**Opportunity cost** is the next best alternative given up. On a PPF it is the output of one good sacrificed to gain another. A **bowed-out** curve shows **increasing opportunity cost**; a straight line shows constant opportunity cost."}, {"h": "Growth and shifts", "b": "**Economic growth** is an outward shift of the PPF, caused by more or better resources, or improved technology. Moving from inside the curve towards it is use of **spare capacity**, not a shift. A fall in capacity shifts the curve inwards."}, {"h": "Capital versus consumer goods", "b": "Choosing more **capital goods** now means fewer **consumer goods** now. Capital raises future productive capacity, so the PPF shifts out further later. The trade-off is current consumption against **future growth**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why an economy that produces more capital goods today may be able to consume more in future.", "steps": [{"do": "Start on the PPF with capital goods on one axis and consumer goods on the other. The economy chooses a point with more capital goods.", "why": "Shows the choice is a movement along the PPF."}, {"do": "This means fewer consumer goods are produced now, because resources are scarce.", "why": "This is the opportunity cost of the choice."}, {"do": "The extra capital goods, such as machinery, raise the economy's productive capacity.", "why": "Links investment to the PPF's determinants."}, {"do": "Over time the PPF shifts outwards, from PPF1 to PPF2.", "why": "Defines economic growth in PPF terms."}, {"do": "So the economy can reach a point with more consumer goods than before, at the cost of lower consumption initially.", "why": "Completes the chain to the conclusion."}], "answer": "Producing more capital goods means fewer consumer goods now, an opportunity cost. Capital raises productive capacity, shifting the PPF outwards. Later the economy can produce more of both goods, so consumption can exceed its earlier level."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the PPF shifts outwards, rather than the economy just moving along it?", "o": ["Because capital raises productive capacity, changing what is attainable", "Because fewer consumer goods are produced", "Because the economy moves inside the curve", "Because opportunity cost is falling"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: more capital increases the maximum output possible, which is a shift of the whole curve.", "Fewer consumer goods now is a movement along the existing curve.", "Moving inside the curve would mean inefficiency, not growth.", "Opportunity cost is not the reason for a shift; changed capacity is."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rise in unemployment moves an economy away from its PPF.", "given": ["Start with the economy producing on its PPF, using all resources efficiently."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Unemployed workers mean resources are idle, so output falls to a point inside the PPF", "The PPF shifts outwards because fewer workers are needed", "The economy moves to a point outside the PPF", "The opportunity cost of every good falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: idle labour means output is below potential, so the economy moves inside the curve.", "Unemployment does not raise capacity; the PPF only shifts if capacity changes.", "Points outside are unattainable, and unemployment reduces output.", "Opportunity cost does not vanish; idle resources just mean output is inefficiently low."]}, "trap": "Do not call a movement towards the PPF, or along it, economic growth. Growth is an outward shift of the whole curve caused by greater capacity.", "recap": "On the PPF means efficient, inside means wasted resources, outside is unattainable, and only an outward shift is economic growth."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "kind": "idea", "why": "Short explain and analyse questions often ask why specialisation raises output, what its costs are, and why money exists. It also underpins trade topics later.", "hook": {"q": "Four friends run a café. Which arrangement will probably serve most customers per hour?", "o": ["Everyone does every job themselves", "Each person focuses on one task: till, drinks, food or cleaning", "Everyone does the same job in turn each hour", "One person does everything while others rest"], "a": 1, "f": ["Switching between jobs wastes time and prevents anyone gaining skill, so output tends to be lower.", "Focusing on one task builds skill and saves switching time, so output per worker usually rises.", "Rotating each hour wastes time switching and loses the gains from repeated practice.", "This wastes the other workers' labour and so cuts total output."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Specialisation and division", "b": "**Specialisation** means focusing on producing a limited range of goods or tasks. The **division of labour** is a form of it: production is split into separate tasks, each done by a different worker."}, {"h": "Why output rises", "b": "Workers repeat one task, so they become faster and more skilled. Less time is lost switching tasks. Capital can be used more fully. Higher **labour productivity** (output per worker) lowers **unit costs** and raises total output."}, {"h": "Drawbacks", "b": "Repetitive work can cause **boredom** and lower motivation. Workers have narrow skills, so they find it hard to move jobs (**occupational immobility**). An economy or region may become **over-dependent** on one industry, and a breakdown at one stage halts production."}, {"h": "Trade and money", "b": "Specialists have a surplus and need other goods, so they **trade**. Barter needs a **double coincidence of wants**. Money solves this as a **medium of exchange**, and also acts as a **store of value**, **unit of account** and **standard of deferred payment**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how specialisation in a bakery can raise output and why this leads to the use of money.", "steps": [{"do": "Define: the bakery divides work so one worker mixes, one bakes and one serves.", "why": "Shows knowledge of division of labour, with context."}, {"do": "Each worker repeats one task, so becomes quicker and more skilled.", "why": "Links the cause to higher labour productivity."}, {"do": "Less time is lost switching between tasks, so more loaves are made per hour.", "why": "Adds a second reason and connects it to output."}, {"do": "The bakery now has a surplus of bread but needs flour, which it does not make, so it must trade.", "why": "Links specialisation to trade through surplus and interdependence."}, {"do": "Barter needs a double coincidence of wants, so money as a medium of exchange makes trade easier.", "why": "Completes the chain to the function of money."}], "answer": "Dividing tasks raises productivity through skill and saved time, so output rises. The surplus must be traded for goods the bakery does not make. Barter needs a double coincidence of wants, so money as a medium of exchange is used."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer link repeated tasks to higher output via labour productivity?", "o": ["Output per worker rises when workers become faster and more skilled", "Wages always rise when tasks are repeated", "Unit costs rise, which increases output", "Workers become less dependent on one another"], "a": 0, "f": ["Repetition builds speed and skill, so each worker produces more, which raises output.", "Wages are not part of this chain and need not rise.", "Unit costs usually fall with higher productivity, not rise.", "Division of labour increases interdependence, not reduces it."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one drawback of the division of labour for a firm making smartphones.", "given": ["Drawback: production is split into narrow tasks, each worker doing only one stage.", "Workers repeat the same task all day."], "q": "What is the best next link in the chain?", "o": ["Repetition causes boredom, lowering motivation and possibly productivity and quality", "Repetition increases skill, so productivity always rises", "Therefore money loses its role as a store of value", "Therefore the firm can barter its phones"], "a": 0, "f": ["Boredom and falling motivation can reduce output and quality, completing the drawback.", "This is a benefit, not a drawback, so it does not answer the question.", "Money's functions are unrelated to repetitive work.", "Bartering is unrelated to worker boredom."]}, "trap": "Do not list only benefits. Name a drawback, link it to the firm or economy in the question, and keep money's four functions separate from specialisation.", "recap": "Specialisation raises productivity and creates surpluses to trade, but brings boredom and dependence; money overcomes the double coincidence of wants in barter."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Economic systems and the views of Smith, Hayek and Marx appear in short-answer definitions and in evaluating the role of the state in essays.", "hook": {"q": "Nobody in the UK plans how many loaves of bread are baked each day. What probably decides it?", "o": ["A government committee", "Consumer demand and firms seeking profit", "A national rationing scheme", "Bakers taking turns"], "a": 1, "f": ["No committee sets bread output; it is left to firms.", "Right: firms respond to prices and demand. This is the price mechanism at work.", "Rationing is not used for bread in normal times.", "Bakers respond to demand, not a rota."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three systems", "b": "A **free market economy** allocates resources through the **price mechanism** with private ownership. A **command economy** uses state planning and ownership. A **mixed economy** combines market allocation with state provision and regulation. Real economies are mixed."}, {"h": "Smith and Hayek", "b": "Smith's **invisible hand**: self-interested individuals in markets promote the wider good without being directed. Hayek: **dispersed knowledge** is scattered among millions, and prices bring it together, which planners cannot do."}, {"h": "Marx's view", "b": "Marx argued capitalism lets owners of capital exploit workers by keeping **surplus value**. He favoured **collective ownership** of the means of production, with the state planning, in place of private ownership."}, {"h": "Role of the state", "b": "In mixed economies the state may provide **public goods**, correct **market failure**, redistribute income and regulate. Evaluation: more intervention can correct failures but risks **government failure** and weaker incentives."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a free market economy may allocate resources more efficiently than a command economy.", "steps": [{"do": "State that in a free market prices signal scarcity and consumer wants.", "why": "Defines the price mechanism, the core of the argument."}, {"do": "Link to firms: rising prices show high demand, so profit-seeking firms produce more of that good.", "why": "Shows how signals change resource allocation."}, {"do": "Add Hayek: the knowledge is dispersed among millions, and prices communicate it.", "why": "Uses an economist's argument to support the point."}, {"do": "Contrast: planners lack this information, so risk shortages or surpluses.", "why": "Makes the comparison the question asks for."}, {"do": "Conclude that profit incentives also push firms to cut costs.", "why": "Adds a second reason for efficiency."}], "answer": "Prices signal scarcity and wants, so profit-seeking firms move resources to where demand is high. Hayek argued the needed knowledge is dispersed and prices communicate it. Planners lack this information and risk shortages or surpluses."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer bring in Hayek's idea of dispersed knowledge?", "o": ["It shows planners earn less profit", "It explains why prices can coordinate information that planners cannot gather", "It proves free markets have no inequality", "It shows prices never change"], "a": 1, "f": ["Hayek's point is about information, not planners' profit.", "Right: it gives the reason prices work better than planning for allocation.", "Hayek did not claim there is no inequality.", "Prices change as information changes; that is the point."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one disadvantage of a free market economy.", "given": ["Free markets rely on the price mechanism and private ownership.", "Some goods, such as street lighting, are public goods."], "q": "What is the best next step?", "o": ["State that firms cannot profitably provide them because non-payers cannot be excluded, so they may be under-provided", "State that prices are fixed by government for these goods", "State that public goods are always produced in excess", "State that free markets cannot provide any private goods"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: non-excludability creates free riders, so the market under-provides, a market failure.", "Fixed prices describe state control, not the free market.", "Public goods tend to be under-provided, not over-provided.", "Free markets do provide private goods; the weakness is elsewhere."]}, "trap": "Do not call any real economy a pure free market or command economy. Write 'mixed' and discuss the degree of state involvement.", "recap": "Free markets use prices, command economies use planning, mixed economies use both; Smith, Hayek and Marx give the arguments."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Rational decision making underpins demand and supply analysis and often appears as a short explain question or as an evaluation point about behavioural economics in longer answers.", "hook": {"q": "You are choosing between two cinema snacks. Which choice would economists call 'rational'?", "o": ["The one giving you the most satisfaction for its cost", "The cheapest one, whatever you enjoy", "The most expensive one, as it must be best", "The one everyone else is buying"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: rational means weighing benefits against costs to get the greatest net satisfaction.", "Wrong: cheapest ignores benefit; rationality compares both sides.", "Wrong: price does not guarantee satisfaction; cost is weighed against benefit.", "Wrong: following others is a behavioural influence, not utility maximisation."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Consumers: maximise utility", "b": "**Utility** is the satisfaction from consuming a good or service. Rational consumers choose the combination of goods that gives the greatest total utility from their limited income. They compare the **marginal utility** of each extra unit with its price."}, {"h": "Firms: maximise profit", "b": "Rational firms aim to maximise **profit**, which is total revenue minus total cost. Profit is greatest where **marginal revenue equals marginal cost** (MR = MC). Producing a unit with MC above MR lowers profit; producing one with MR above MC raises it."}, {"h": "Governments and workers", "b": "Governments are assumed to maximise **social welfare**, weighing the costs and benefits to society. Workers are assumed to maximise **welfare** by balancing wages against leisure and working conditions. In each case the decision-maker weighs costs against benefits."}, {"h": "Limits of the assumption", "b": "Real behaviour may differ. Consumers may follow **habit**, be influenced by others or lack **information**. Firms may pursue sales or growth instead of profit. This is why **behavioural economics** challenges the rationality assumption."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a firm assumed to be a rational profit maximiser stops raising output at the point where MR = MC.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the firm's aim: a rational firm seeks to maximise profit, the gap between total revenue and total cost.", "why": "Starting with the objective frames the whole argument."}, {"do": "State that each extra unit adds marginal revenue (MR) to revenue and marginal cost (MC) to cost.", "why": "Profit changes at the margin, so MR and MC are the relevant terms."}, {"do": "If MR exceeds MC, the extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so profit rises and the firm expands output.", "why": "This shows why it should not stop before MR = MC."}, {"do": "If MC exceeds MR, the extra unit lowers profit, so the firm would cut output.", "why": "This shows why it should not go beyond MR = MC."}, {"do": "So profit is maximised where MR = MC, as no change in output can then add to profit.", "why": "The conclusion links the two earlier points directly to the question."}], "answer": "Profit is maximised at MR = MC because below that output extra units add more to revenue than cost, and above it they add more cost than revenue."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example consider what happens when MC exceeds MR?", "o": ["It shows that producing beyond MR = MC would reduce profit", "It shows that firms always want lower costs", "It proves that revenue falls as output rises", "It shows that consumers maximise utility"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: if MC is above MR, the extra unit reduces profit, so output beyond that point is not rational.", "Wrong: the aim is maximum profit, not lowest cost; the comparison is of MC with MR.", "Wrong: MR may still be positive; the point is that cost rises by more.", "Wrong: this step concerns firms, not consumers."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rational consumer might stop buying more of a good even though it still gives some satisfaction.", "given": ["Rational consumers aim to maximise utility from limited income.", "Each extra unit gives marginal utility, which tends to fall as more is consumed."], "q": "What is the next point?", "o": ["Buy until the marginal utility of an extra unit no longer justifies its price", "Buy until marginal utility is as high as possible", "Buy until income is fully spent on this good", "Buy until the price falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the consumer compares marginal utility with price and stops when extra satisfaction is worth less than the cost.", "Wrong: marginal utility falls as consumption rises, so it is not maximised by buying more.", "Wrong: spending all income on one good ignores the utility from other goods.", "Wrong: price is given to the consumer; they respond to it rather than choosing it."]}, "trap": "Do not say rational means selfish or always right. It means weighing costs and benefits to meet one's aims given the information held, which may be imperfect.", "recap": "Rational agents weigh costs against benefits: consumers maximise utility, firms maximise profit at MR = MC, though real behaviour may differ."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Distinguishing a movement along from a shift of the demand curve is tested in multiple-choice, data response and diagram-based explanations throughout Papers 1 and 3.", "hook": {"q": "A bakery cuts the price of its sourdough loaf. More loaves are sold. Has the demand curve for sourdough moved?", "o": ["Yes, it has shifted right", "Yes, it has shifted left", "No, we have moved along the same curve", "No, because quantity demanded did not change"], "a": 2, "f": ["Cheaper sourdough raises quantity demanded, but price is on the axis, so the curve stays put.", "Wrong direction, and a price change does not shift the curve anyway.", "Price is on the vertical axis, so its change is shown as a movement along the curve.", "Quantity demanded did rise; that is the movement along the curve."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Movement or shift?", "b": "A change in the good's own **price** causes a **movement along** the demand curve: an **extension** if price falls, a **contraction** if price rises. Any other influence changes demand at every price and causes a **shift** of the curve."}, {"h": "Conditions of demand", "b": "The **conditions of demand** are: real income, tastes and preferences (including advertising), prices of **substitutes** and **complements**, population size and structure, and expectations. A rise in demand shifts the curve right; a fall shifts it left."}, {"h": "Related goods and income", "b": "If a substitute's price rises, demand for this good rises. If a complement's price rises, demand for this good falls. Demand for a **normal good** rises with income; for an **inferior good** it falls as income rises."}, {"h": "Diminishing marginal utility", "b": "**Utility** is satisfaction. **Marginal utility** is the extra satisfaction from one more unit. It falls as consumption rises: **diminishing marginal utility**. So consumers will only buy more if price falls, which explains the downward-sloping demand curve."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a fall in the price of games consoles may shift the demand curve for video games to the right.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the relationship: consoles and video games are complements, used together.", "why": "Naming the type of related good is the knowledge mark."}, {"do": "State that cheaper consoles extend the quantity of consoles demanded, so more consoles are owned.", "why": "Shows the movement along the console demand curve first."}, {"do": "Link: more console owners want more games at any given game price.", "why": "This is the chain of reasoning that earns analysis marks."}, {"do": "Conclude that demand for games rises at every price, so the curve shifts right.", "why": "A shift, not a movement, because the game's own price has not changed."}], "answer": "Consoles and games are complements. Cheaper consoles mean more are bought, so more people want games at every price. A change in a condition of demand, not the game's own price, so the curve shifts right."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the shift in the games curve not described as an extension of demand?", "o": ["The game's own price has not changed, so it is not a movement along", "Extensions only happen to normal goods", "Games are inferior goods, so no extension occurs", "Extensions apply only to complements"], "a": 0, "f": ["Extensions come from a change in the good's own price; here the cause is a related good's price.", "Extensions apply to any good with a falling own price, not just normal goods.", "Nothing suggests games are inferior goods, and the type of good is irrelevant here.", "Extensions are not limited to complements; they depend on own price."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a rise in the price of butter may shift the demand curve for margarine to the right.", "given": ["Butter and margarine are substitutes."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain?", "o": ["Dearer butter makes margarine relatively cheaper, so consumers switch towards margarine", "Dearer butter lowers margarine's price, extending demand along the curve", "Dearer butter reduces incomes, so demand for margarine as a normal good falls", "Dearer butter makes consumers buy more of both goods at every price"], "a": 0, "f": ["Switching to the relatively cheaper substitute leads to more margarine demanded at every price.", "Margarine's own price has not changed, so no extension occurs.", "The link is substitution, not income, and a fall would shift the curve left.", "Substitutes move in opposite directions, so buying more of both is wrong."]}, "trap": "Saying a price change shifts the curve. Own price moves you along it; only conditions of demand shift it. Check what has changed before choosing.", "recap": "Own price changes move you along the demand curve; changes in conditions of demand shift the whole curve."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "kind": "method", "why": "Elasticity calculations and their interpretation appear in Paper 1 and 3 data-response questions, and the results support evaluation of pricing and tax decisions.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The three formulas", "b": "**PED** = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP. **YED** = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY (income). **XED** = %ΔQd of good A ÷ %ΔP of good B. Always convert changes into percentages first: change ÷ original × 100. Keep the sign."}, {"h": "Reading the value", "b": "PED: beyond −1 (magnitude above 1) is **elastic**; between 0 and −1 is **inelastic**. YED: positive means a **normal good**, negative means an **inferior good**; above +1 is income elastic. XED: positive means **substitutes**, negative means **complements**; larger magnitude means a closer link."}, {"h": "PED and revenue", "b": "Revenue = price × quantity. If demand is **elastic**, a price rise lowers revenue and a price cut raises it. If demand is **inelastic**, a price rise raises revenue and a price cut lowers it. At unit elasticity revenue does not change."}, {"h": "Determinants and taxes", "b": "PED depends on the number and closeness of **substitutes**, whether the good is a necessity, its share of income, and the time period (demand is more elastic over longer periods). Governments tax goods with **inelastic demand** to raise revenue, since quantity falls little."}], "example": {"task": "Price falls from £5.00 to £4.50 and quantity demanded rises from 200 to 230. Calculate PED, say what it shows, and state the effect on revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "%ΔP = (4.50 − 5.00) ÷ 5.00 × 100 = −10%.", "why": "Use the percentage change from the original price; a fall gives a negative figure."}, {"do": "%ΔQd = (230 − 200) ÷ 200 × 100 = +15%.", "why": "Same method for quantity, using the original quantity as the base."}, {"do": "PED = +15 ÷ −10 = −1.5.", "why": "Divide %ΔQd by %ΔP and keep the sign; it is negative as expected."}, {"do": "Demand is price elastic, as the magnitude 1.5 is greater than 1.", "why": "Quantity changes proportionately more than price."}, {"do": "Revenue before = £5 × 200 = £1,000. After = £4.50 × 230 = £1,035. Revenue rises by £35.", "why": "This confirms the rule that a price cut raises revenue when demand is elastic."}], "answer": "PED = −1.5, so demand is elastic. The price cut raises revenue from £1,000 to £1,035."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does revenue rise after the price cut in the worked example?", "o": ["Price fell, and a lower price always raises revenue", "Quantity rose by 15%, more than the 10% fall in price", "Quantity demanded rose by 30 units, which is more than the price fell", "The PED is negative, so revenue must rise"], "a": 1, "f": ["A lower price does not always raise revenue; it depends on the PED.", "Right: the percentage rise in quantity exceeds the percentage fall in price, which is what elastic demand means, so P × Q rises.", "Comparing units with pounds is meaningless; elasticity compares percentage changes.", "PED is usually negative, so the sign cannot decide the effect. The size relative to 1 does."]}, "faded": {"task": "Income rises from £1,500 to £1,800. Monthly quantity of a good falls from 50 to 45 units. Calculate YED and say what type of good it is.", "given": ["%ΔY = 300 ÷ 1,500 × 100 = +20%", "%ΔQd = −5 ÷ 50 × 100 = −10%"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["YED = −10 ÷ 20 = +0.5, a normal good", "YED = 20 ÷ −10 = −2.0, an inferior good", "YED = −10 ÷ 20 = −0.5, an inferior good", "YED = −10 ÷ 20 = −0.5, a normal good"], "a": 2, "f": ["Dropped the sign: quantity fell as income rose, so YED is negative.", "You inverted the formula; %ΔQd goes on top, giving −0.5, not −2.0.", "Right: YED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY = −0.5. A negative YED means demand falls as income rises, so it is an inferior good.", "The value is right, but a negative YED means an inferior good, not a normal one."]}, "trap": "Using absolute changes instead of percentages, or ignoring the sign. Always work out % change from the original value, and read the sign to name the type of good.", "recap": "Elasticity is %ΔQd divided by the % change in price, income or another good's price; the sign and size tell you the interpretation."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Supply questions often test whether you can tell a movement from a shift. Diagram and explanation marks depend on it.", "hook": {"q": "A baker's costs of flour rise. Which is more likely to happen to the number of loaves she is willing to supply at £1 each?", "o": ["It rises", "It falls", "It stays the same", "It cannot be known"], "a": 1, "f": ["Higher costs cut profit per loaf, so she would not supply more at the same price.", "Right: higher costs reduce profit at each price, so she supplies fewer loaves at £1.", "Costs affect profit margins, so supply at each price would change.", "The direction is clear: higher costs reduce willingness to supply."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Movement along the curve", "b": "A change in the good's **own price** causes a movement along the **supply curve**. A higher price gives an **extension** of supply. A lower price gives a **contraction**. The curve itself does not move."}, {"h": "Shifts of the curve", "b": "A change in a **condition of supply** shifts the curve. A rightward shift is an **increase in supply**: more is supplied at every price. A leftward shift is a **decrease in supply**: less is supplied at every price."}, {"h": "The conditions of supply", "b": "These include **costs of production**, **technology**, **indirect taxes**, **subsidies**, the number of firms, producers' objectives, and weather or other shocks. Costs up, taxes up, or fewer firms shift supply left. Subsidies and better technology shift it right."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a rise in the cost of raw materials affects the supply of chocolate bars.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the factor: raw material costs are a condition of supply.", "why": "It is not the bar's own price, so it shifts the curve."}, {"do": "State that higher costs of production reduce profit per bar at each price.", "why": "This links the cause to firms' behaviour."}, {"do": "Firms are willing to supply fewer bars at every price.", "why": "This is the meaning of a decrease in supply."}, {"do": "The supply curve shifts left, from S1 to S2.", "why": "Shows the diagram effect correctly as a shift."}, {"do": "Conclude: the equilibrium price rises and quantity falls, assuming demand is unchanged.", "why": "Links the shift to the market outcome."}], "answer": "Higher raw material costs reduce profit per bar at each price, so supply decreases and the curve shifts left. Equilibrium price rises and quantity falls."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example show a shift left rather than a contraction along the curve?", "o": ["Because the cause is a change in cost, not the bar's own price", "Because firms always supply less when prices rise", "Because contractions only happen with taxes", "Because a leftward shift needs a fall in demand"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: cost is a condition of supply, so it moves the whole curve.", "Firms supply more when own price rises; that would be an extension.", "Contractions come from a fall in own price, not taxes.", "Demand does not cause supply to shift."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a new subsidy for wind farms affects the supply of electricity from wind.", "given": ["Identify the factor: a subsidy is a condition of supply.", "It lowers the costs of production per unit."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Wind farms supply more at every price, so the supply curve shifts right", "Wind farms supply more only at higher prices, so supply extends along the curve", "Demand for wind power falls, shifting the demand curve left", "Wind farms supply less because profits fall"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: lower costs mean more is supplied at every price, shifting supply right.", "An extension needs a rise in own price; the subsidy is a different cause.", "A subsidy affects firms' costs, not demand.", "A subsidy raises profit per unit, so supply rises, not falls."]}, "trap": "Treating a cost change as a movement along the curve. Ask: is the cause the good's own price? If not, the curve shifts.", "recap": "Own price moves you along the supply curve; every other condition of supply shifts the whole curve."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "kind": "method", "why": "PES appears in calculation questions and in 'explain' and 'evaluate' answers on price changes, often alongside PED, with marks for the time-period point.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "The PES formula", "b": "**Price elasticity of supply** measures the responsiveness of **quantity supplied** to a change in price. $PES=\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_s}{\\%\\Delta P}$. It is positive for normal supply curves. Use percentage changes, not absolute ones."}, {"h": "Interpreting values", "b": "If PES > 1, supply is **elastic**. If PES < 1, it is **inelastic**. PES = 1 is **unit elastic**, 0 is perfectly inelastic (vertical), and infinite is perfectly elastic (horizontal). A straight line from the origin always has PES = 1."}, {"h": "Determinants of PES", "b": "PES is higher with **spare capacity**, **stocks**, mobile factors of production, easy entry for new firms and a short production time. It is lower when capacity is full, inputs are scarce or specialised, or stocks are perishable."}, {"h": "Short run and long run", "b": "In the **short run** at least one factor is fixed, so supply is less elastic. In the **long run** all factors can change and new firms can enter, so supply is more elastic. Exam answers should link time to this."}], "example": {"task": "A farm's price per tonne of wheat rises from £150 to £180. Quantity supplied rises from 400 to 460 tonnes. Calculate PES and interpret it.", "steps": [{"do": "Find %ΔP: (180 − 150) ÷ 150 × 100 = 20%.", "why": "Change divided by the original price, as a percentage."}, {"do": "Find %ΔQs: (460 − 400) ÷ 400 × 100 = 15%.", "why": "Same method for quantity, using the original quantity."}, {"do": "PES = 15 ÷ 20 = 0.75.", "why": "Apply %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP with quantity on top."}, {"do": "Interpret: 0.75 is below 1, so supply is inelastic.", "why": "Quantity supplied rises proportionately less than price."}], "answer": "PES = 0.75. Supply is price inelastic: a 20% price rise leads to only a 15% rise in quantity supplied, perhaps because farmland and growing time are fixed."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the worked example divide by the original values (150 and 400)?", "o": ["Percentage change is measured relative to the starting value", "The new values are always less reliable", "PES must be less than 1", "It removes the need to use percentages"], "a": 0, "f": ["Percentage change is the change as a share of the starting point.", "Reliability is not the reason; the method is a convention for percentage change.", "PES can take any positive value; the result depends on the data.", "The method still uses percentages; it just fixes the base."]}, "faded": {"task": "A toy shop's supplier raises its price from £8 to £10 per toy. Quantity supplied rises from 200 to 250 toys.", "given": ["%ΔP = (10 − 8) ÷ 8 × 100 = 25%"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 250 × 100 = 20%", "%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 200 × 100 = 25%, so PES = 25 ÷ 25 = 1", "%ΔQs = 50 ÷ 200 × 100 = 25%, so PES = 25 × 25", "%ΔQs = 250 ÷ 200 = 125%, so PES = 5"], "a": 1, "f": ["This divides by the new quantity; use the original, 200.", "Correct: 50 ÷ 200 = 25%, then 25 ÷ 25 = 1, so supply is unit elastic.", "Multiplying the percentages is wrong; PES is a ratio, so divide.", "This treats the new quantity as a percentage of the old, not the change."]}, "trap": "Do not use absolute changes or divide the wrong way round. Put quantity supplied on top, use the original values as the base, and remember PES is positive.", "recap": "PES = %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP; supply is more elastic with spare capacity, stocks and more time, so long-run supply is more elastic."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "kind": "idea", "why": "Price determination underpins almost every diagram question in Papers 1 and 3, and your analysis chains rely on shifting curves correctly.", "hook": {"q": "A shop has too many unsold items at its current price. What would you expect it to do?", "o": ["Raise the price", "Lower the price", "Keep the price the same", "Stop selling the item entirely"], "a": 1, "f": ["Raising price would reduce sales further and worsen the surplus.", "Right: surplus stock pushes sellers to cut price until sales match stock. This is how markets reach equilibrium.", "Leaving price unchanged leaves the surplus in place.", "Stopping sales is extreme; price cuts usually clear the surplus first."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Equilibrium price", "b": "**Equilibrium** is where **quantity demanded equals quantity supplied**. Demand and supply curves cross here. The price is the **market clearing price**: there is no tendency to change, as no buyer or seller is frustrated."}, {"h": "Disequilibrium", "b": "Above equilibrium, **excess supply** (a surplus) exists, so price falls. Below it, **excess demand** (a shortage) exists, so price rises. These price changes are **movements along** the curves, which restore equilibrium. This is the **price mechanism** at work."}, {"h": "Shifts of curves", "b": "A change in a **non-price factor** (income, tastes, costs, taxes) **shifts** a curve. This creates disequilibrium at the old price. Price then adjusts, causing an **extension or contraction** along the other curve, to a new equilibrium."}, {"h": "Direction rules", "b": "Demand right: price up, quantity up. Demand left: both down. Supply right: price down, quantity up. Supply left: price up, quantity down. When both shift, one of price or quantity is certain; the other depends on **relative size**."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in the cost of flour affects the equilibrium price and quantity of bread in a UK market.", "steps": [{"do": "Flour is a cost of production, so higher flour costs reduce profitability at every price.", "why": "Identifies a non-price supply factor, not a price change."}, {"do": "Supply shifts left, from S1 to S2.", "why": "Firms supply less at each price, so the curve moves leftwards."}, {"do": "At the old price there is excess demand, as Qd exceeds the new Qs.", "why": "Describes disequilibrium created by the shift."}, {"do": "The shortage bids price up.", "why": "The price mechanism rations the scarce bread."}, {"do": "As price rises, quantity demanded contracts along D and quantity supplied extends along S2, reaching a new equilibrium with higher price and lower quantity.", "why": "Shows the movements along curves and the final result."}], "answer": "Higher flour costs shift supply left, creating excess demand at the old price. Price rises, causing a contraction in demand and extension in supply along S2. The new equilibrium has a higher price and lower quantity."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer describe a contraction of demand rather than a shift of demand?", "o": ["Demand curves never shift", "The fall in quantity demanded is caused by the price rise, a change in price", "Flour is not related to bread demand", "Contractions happen only when supply shifts right"], "a": 1, "f": ["Demand curves do shift when non-price factors change, such as income.", "Right: a change in the good's own price causes a movement along the curve, not a shift.", "Flour affects supply costs; the point is about what moves demand, not relatedness.", "Contraction follows a price rise, which here comes from a left shift of supply."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a fall in consumers' incomes affects the market for a normal good such as new cars.", "given": ["Income is a non-price factor affecting demand.", "Demand shifts left, from D1 to D2."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["Excess supply exists at the old price, so price falls", "Excess demand exists at the old price, so price rises", "Supply shifts right to meet the lower demand", "Price stays the same but quantity falls"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: with lower demand, Qs exceeds Qd at the old price, so price falls.", "Excess demand would follow a rightward demand shift, not a leftward one.", "Supply does not shift; income is a demand factor. Supply contracts along its curve.", "Price adjusts through the price mechanism; it does not stay fixed."]}, "trap": "Do not shift a curve because price changed. Only non-price factors shift curves; the price change moves you along the other curve to the new equilibrium.", "recap": "Non-price changes shift a curve, creating excess demand or supply; price then adjusts along the other curve to a new equilibrium."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 1 and 3 questions ask you to explain how prices allocate resources, usually by applying rationing, incentive and signalling to a market change.", "hook": {"q": "Concert tickets for a popular band sell out fast and resale prices soar. What is the price doing?", "o": ["Showing demand is high and deterring some buyers", "Making demand rise further", "Reducing the band's incentive to play more shows", "Having no effect on who gets tickets"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the high price signals strong demand and deters buyers who are not willing or able to pay.", "A higher price does not raise demand; it reduces quantity demanded along the curve.", "High prices raise the incentive to supply more shows, not reduce it.", "Price does decide who gets tickets, as only those willing and able to pay will buy."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Rationing", "b": "Resources are **scarce**, so not everyone can have what they want. A rising price reduces **quantity demanded**. Only consumers **willing and able to pay** get the good. Rationing works when demand exceeds supply at the current price."}, {"h": "Incentives", "b": "Higher prices raise **profit**, giving firms an **incentive** to supply more. Lower prices cut profit and give an incentive to produce less. Consumers also respond: a price rise gives them an incentive to buy substitutes."}, {"h": "Signalling", "b": "Price changes act as **signals** about where resources are wanted. A rise after a demand shift signals producers to **reallocate resources** into that market. A fall signals resources should leave. This happens without central planning."}, {"h": "Linking to shifts", "b": "Start with a shift in **demand** or **supply**. This creates **excess demand or supply** at the old price. Price then adjusts to a new equilibrium, and the three functions explain how the market responds."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the price mechanism allocates resources when demand for plant-based burgers increases.", "steps": [{"do": "Demand shifts right, creating excess demand at the original price.", "why": "Starts with the cause, a shift in demand, as the analysis must begin with a change."}, {"do": "Price rises, so some consumers are priced out. Quantity demanded contracts along the new demand curve.", "why": "This is rationing: price allocates scarce burgers to those willing and able to pay."}, {"do": "Higher price and profit give producers an incentive to supply more.", "why": "Incentive function: profit motive drives increased supply."}, {"do": "The higher price signals to firms that resources should move into plant-based burgers from less profitable uses.", "why": "Signalling function: resources are reallocated without central direction."}, {"do": "Supply extends until a new equilibrium is reached where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.", "why": "Shows the market clearing, completing the chain."}], "answer": "Demand rises, causing excess demand. Price rises, rationing burgers to those willing and able to pay. Higher profit gives firms an incentive to supply more and signals resources should move here, until a new equilibrium."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say quantity demanded contracts along the demand curve, rather than demand falling?", "o": ["Because the cause of the change is price, which moves us along the curve", "Because the demand curve shifts left when price rises", "Because consumers' incomes have fallen", "Because supply has also shifted left"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: a change in the good's own price causes a movement along the demand curve, not a shift.", "A price rise does not shift demand; shifts come from non-price factors.", "Nothing says income changed; the cause is the price rise.", "Supply does not need to shift; the price rise comes from the demand shift."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how the price mechanism responds when a poor harvest reduces the supply of wheat.", "given": ["Supply shifts left, causing excess demand at the original price.", "Price rises."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain of explanation?", "o": ["Rationing: quantity demanded contracts as higher price deters some buyers", "Demand shifts right because wheat is now scarcer", "Producers face weaker incentives so all leave the market", "The price falls to restore equilibrium"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the higher price rations the limited wheat by reducing quantity demanded along the demand curve.", "Scarcity does not shift demand; price changes cause movements along the curve.", "Higher price raises profit incentives for remaining and new suppliers, not weakens them.", "Price needs to rise, not fall, to remove excess demand after a supply fall."]}, "trap": "Do not just say price rises or falls. Name the function, rationing, incentive or signalling, and link it to a shift and a movement along a curve.", "recap": "Prices ration scarce goods, create incentives through profit, and signal where resources should move after a shift in demand or supply."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "kind": "method", "why": "Short calculation and diagram questions ask you to find or compare surplus areas, and longer answers use surplus to judge who gains or loses when a market changes.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What the surpluses are", "b": "**Consumer surplus** is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay and the price they actually pay. **Producer surplus** is the difference between the price producers receive and the minimum price they would accept. Both are measured in £."}, {"h": "Finding the areas", "b": "**Consumer surplus** is the area under the demand curve and above the price, up to the quantity traded. **Producer surplus** is the area above the supply curve and below the price. With straight lines each is a triangle: ½ × quantity × price gap."}, {"h": "Shifts change the areas", "b": "A shift in demand or supply moves **equilibrium** price and quantity, so both areas change. Cheaper price with more sold usually enlarges **consumer surplus**. A higher price and more sold enlarges **producer surplus**. Recalculate both triangles to compare."}], "example": {"task": "Demand is P = 24 − 2Q and supply is P = 4 + 2Q (P in £, Q in units). Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium.", "steps": [{"do": "Set demand equal to supply: 24 − 2Q = 4 + 2Q, so 20 = 4Q and Q = 5. Then P = 24 − 2(5) = £14.", "why": "Surpluses are measured at the equilibrium price and quantity."}, {"do": "Read the intercepts: demand meets the price axis at £24 and supply at £4.", "why": "These are the tops and bottoms of the triangles."}, {"do": "Consumer surplus = ½ × 5 × (24 − 14) = ½ × 5 × 10 = £25.", "why": "Height is the gap from the demand intercept to the price, not the price."}, {"do": "Producer surplus = ½ × 5 × (14 − 4) = ½ × 5 × 10 = £25.", "why": "Height is the gap from the price down to the supply intercept."}], "answer": "Equilibrium is Q = 5 and P = £14. Consumer surplus is £25 and producer surplus is £25, so total surplus is £50."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is the height of the consumer surplus triangle 24 − 14 and not 24?", "o": ["Consumers only gain the amount above the price they actually pay", "The price axis starts at £14 on this diagram", "Demand and supply must both be subtracted to find any triangle", "Consumer surplus is measured from the supply intercept upwards"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right: the surplus is willingness to pay minus price paid, so the triangle sits between the demand curve and the price line.", "The axis starts at zero; the £14 is the equilibrium price, which forms the triangle's base line.", "Only the price is subtracted for consumer surplus; supply's intercept is used for producer surplus.", "The supply intercept (£4) is the lower edge of producer surplus, not of consumer surplus."]}, "faded": {"task": "Demand is P = 40 − 4Q and supply is P = 10 + 2Q (£, units). Calculate consumer surplus at equilibrium.", "given": ["Set equal: 40 − 4Q = 10 + 2Q, so 30 = 6Q and Q = 5.", "Price: P = 40 − 4(5) = £20.", "Demand intercept is £40; supply intercept is £10."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["CS = ½ × 5 × 40 = £100", "CS = 5 × (40 − 20) = £100", "CS = ½ × 5 × (20 − 10) = £25", "CS = ½ × 5 × (40 − 20) = £50"], "a": 3, "f": ["The height must be the gap 40 − 20, not the full £40 intercept.", "This leaves out the ½; a triangle's area is half base times height.", "This is producer surplus, using the supply intercept; consumer surplus uses the demand intercept.", "Right: base 5 units, height is 40 − 20 = £20, so ½ × 5 × 20 = £50."]}, "trap": "Using the full price or intercept as the height, or forgetting the ½. Always subtract: demand intercept minus price for CS; price minus supply intercept for PS.", "recap": "Surplus is a triangle, ½ × quantity × price gap, found at equilibrium; when a curve shifts, recalculate both areas."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "kind": "method", "why": "Indirect tax and subsidy diagrams appear in data-response and calculation questions, often asking you to find who bears a tax, or what it costs the government.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Shifting the supply curve", "b": "A per-unit **indirect tax** raises costs, so **supply** shifts vertically up by the amount of the tax. A **subsidy** lowers costs, so supply shifts vertically down by the subsidy. Demand does not move. Read the new price and quantity at the new equilibrium."}, {"h": "Splitting the tax", "b": "The tax is the vertical gap between the two supply curves. **Consumer burden** = (new price − old price) × new quantity. **Producer burden** = (tax − price rise) × new quantity. Producers receive the new price minus the tax."}, {"h": "Revenue and subsidy cost", "b": "**Tax revenue** = tax per unit × new quantity. It equals consumer burden plus producer burden. For a subsidy, **government spending** = subsidy per unit × new quantity. The price fall is the consumers' gain per unit; the rest of the subsidy is the producers' gain."}, {"h": "Why elasticity matters", "b": "The more **inelastic** demand is, the more of a tax consumers bear. The more inelastic supply is, the more producers bear. Inelastic demand also means quantity falls less, so tax revenue is higher."}], "example": {"task": "A £4 per-unit tax moves equilibrium from price £15.50, quantity 450, to price £16.50, quantity 350. Find the consumer burden, producer burden and tax revenue.", "steps": [{"do": "Find the price rise: £16.50 − £15.50 = £1.00.", "why": "This is how much more each consumer pays per unit."}, {"do": "Consumer burden = £1.00 × 350 = £350.", "why": "Multiply by the new quantity, since tax is paid only on units still sold."}, {"do": "Producer share per unit = £4.00 − £1.00 = £3.00. Producer burden = £3.00 × 350 = £1,050.", "why": "Firms absorb the part of the tax not passed on in the price."}, {"do": "Tax revenue = £4 × 350 = £1,400.", "why": "Revenue is tax per unit times new quantity."}, {"do": "Check: £350 + £1,050 = £1,400.", "why": "The two burdens must add up to the revenue."}], "answer": "Consumers bear £350, producers bear £1,050 and the government raises £1,400. Producers bear more because only a quarter of the tax (£1 of £4) is passed on in a higher price, which suggests demand is more elastic than supply."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why is producer burden calculated using the new quantity of 350, not the old quantity of 450?", "o": ["Because the tax is paid only on units still bought and sold after the tax", "Because the old quantity is only used for subsidies", "Because producers always sell exactly the new quantity at the old price", "Because demand has shifted left to 350 after the tax"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["Right. The 100 lost units are not sold, so no tax is paid on them. All incidence areas use the new quantity.", "Wrong. The old quantity is not used for subsidies either; subsidy cost also uses the new quantity.", "Wrong. Producers receive less than the old price per unit after the tax, and the quantity is lower because of the higher price.", "Wrong. Demand has not shifted. Supply shifted up, and quantity fell by a movement along the demand curve."]}, "faded": {"task": "A £3 per-unit subsidy lowers the price from £8.00 to £6.50 and raises quantity from 200 to 260. Find the gain to producers.", "given": ["Government cost = £3 × 260 = £780.", "Consumers' gain per unit = £8.00 − £6.50 = £1.50."], "q": "What is the next step to find the total gain to producers?", "o": ["(£3.00 + £1.50) × 260 = £1,170", "£3.00 × 260 = £780", "(£3.00 − £1.50) × 260 = £390", "(£8.00 − £6.50) × 200 = £300"], "a": 2, "f": ["Wrong. The consumers' £1.50 is part of the £3 subsidy, not extra on top of it. Subtract it from £3.00 to get the producers' share.", "Wrong. £780 is the whole subsidy, which consumers and producers share. Subtract the consumers' £1.50 first.", "Right. Producers gain the part of the subsidy not passed on in the price: £1.50 per unit, times the new quantity of 260.", "Wrong. This is the consumers' gain per unit, and it uses the old quantity. Producers' share is the rest of the subsidy."]}, "trap": "Using the old quantity, or treating the whole tax as paid by consumers. Always use the new quantity, then check that the burdens add to tax revenue.", "recap": "Tax or subsidy per unit × new quantity gives the total; the price change gives the consumers' share and the remainder is the producers'."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "kind": "idea", "why": "Behavioural economics appears in short explain questions and in evaluation of rational-choice theory, so you need to name each influence and link it to demand.", "hook": {"q": "You choose a restaurant because it has a long queue outside, without reading any reviews. Why might you do this?", "o": ["You assume others know something you do not", "You have calculated the exact utility of each meal", "You are following your own past choices", "You have perfect information about every restaurant"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: you treat other people's choices as a signal of quality. This is the idea behind herd behaviour.", "Wrong: you have not calculated anything, since you skipped reviews.", "Wrong: this is not a repeat of your own past choice; it is a new restaurant.", "Wrong: if you had perfect information, you would not need the queue as a signal."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Rational choice benchmark", "b": "Traditional theory assumes consumers act **rationally** to maximise **utility**, using full information and weighing costs and benefits. **Behavioural economics** studies how real people depart from this, which affects how demand behaves."}, {"h": "Other people's choices", "b": "**Herd behaviour** means copying what others do, often because their choices seem to signal quality or safety. **Social norms** also shape what people buy. Demand can rise or fall sharply without any change in price or real quality."}, {"h": "Habit", "b": "**Habitual behaviour** means repeating past choices automatically, without comparing alternatives. It saves effort, but consumers may pay more than necessary and respond less to price changes, so demand is less **price elastic**."}, {"h": "Difficulty with calculations", "b": "Comparing complex options is hard, so people use **rules of thumb**, such as choosing the lowest headline price. This is **bounded rationality**: limited ability to process information. Consumers may then miss the best-value choice."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why many consumers buy a popular smartphone even when a cheaper phone offers the same features.", "steps": [{"do": "Say that traditional theory predicts rational consumers choosing the cheaper phone for the same utility.", "why": "This sets the benchmark the behaviour departs from."}, {"do": "Identify herd behaviour: consumers copy others, assuming popularity signals quality.", "why": "This names the specific influence of other people's choices."}, {"do": "Add habit: owners of earlier models of the brand simply buy the next one without comparing.", "why": "A second factor shows depth, and habit is a distinct concept."}, {"do": "Add difficulty with calculations: comparing specifications and contract costs is hard, so people use the brand as a shortcut.", "why": "This links to bounded rationality and rules of thumb."}, {"do": "Conclude that demand for the popular phone is higher and less price sensitive than rational theory predicts.", "why": "The final link to demand turns description into explanation."}], "answer": "Rational consumers would buy the cheaper phone. Herd behaviour, habit and difficulty comparing options mean many buy the popular brand, so its demand is higher and less price elastic than theory predicts."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example end by linking the behaviour to demand?", "o": ["Because the question asks for the exact value of elasticity", "Because explanations must show the effect on the market, not just name the behaviour", "Because herd behaviour only affects supply", "Because habit always makes demand perfectly inelastic"], "a": 1, "x": "", "f": ["Wrong: no calculation is needed; the link is a qualitative effect.", "Right: naming a concept earns little. Linking it to what happens to demand completes the chain of reasoning.", "Wrong: herd behaviour changes consumer demand, not supply.", "Wrong: habit makes demand less elastic, not perfectly inelastic."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why many households stay with the same bank account for years despite better offers elsewhere.", "given": ["Rational consumers would compare offers and switch to the one giving the most utility.", "Habit means households keep using the same account without reviewing alternatives."], "q": "Which is the best next step?", "o": ["Add that herd behaviour means households choose a bank because of its low price.", "Add that comparing interest rates, fees and conditions is complex, so people rely on rules of thumb or avoid comparing.", "State that the households have perfect information but dislike switching.", "State that the banks must have the same prices, so there is no benefit to switching."], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong: herd behaviour is about copying others, and price is not the cause here.", "Right: this adds difficulty with calculations, a second distinct factor that explains the inertia.", "Wrong: perfect information contradicts the idea of behavioural limits.", "Wrong: this assumes away the 'better offers' stated in the question."]}, "trap": "Do not just list behaviours. Name the behaviour, explain it in the context, then say how it makes demand differ from the rational-choice prediction.", "recap": "People often follow others, repeat habits and use shortcuts for hard calculations, so demand departs from rational-choice predictions."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Market failure underpins all of theme 1.3, and short-answer questions often ask you to define it or identify which type of failure a scenario shows.", "hook": {"q": "A shop on your street pays to put up bright lights. Everyone walking past benefits. What is the likeliest problem for the shop?", "o": ["It cannot easily charge passers-by, though they benefit", "It will be forced to cut its prices", "Passers-by will use the lights up, leaving none for others", "The lights will raise the shop's costs of production"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: people benefit without paying. This is the free-rider idea behind missing markets and externalities.", "Price cuts have nothing to do with it; the problem is non-payment by beneficiaries.", "Light is not used up by others, so it is non-rival; the problem is not scarcity of the light.", "The shop does bear costs, but the key issue is that benefits go to others who do not pay."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What market failure means", "b": "**Market failure** occurs when the free market leads to a misallocation of resources, so **social welfare** is not maximised. Prices fail to reflect the full costs or benefits to society, or a market is missing altogether."}, {"h": "Externalities", "b": "An **externality** is a cost or benefit to a third party, outside the market transaction. **MSC = MPC + marginal external cost**; **MSB = MPB + marginal external benefit**. Negative externalities cause over-production or over-consumption; positive ones cause under-provision."}, {"h": "Public goods", "b": "A **public good** is **non-rival** and **non-excludable**. Non-excludability lets people **free-ride**, so firms cannot charge. The market does not provide the good, a missing market. Examples: street lighting, national defence."}, {"h": "Information gaps", "b": "**Asymmetric information** means one party knows more than the other. Consumers may misjudge the benefits or costs of a good, such as underestimating the harm of smoking. They then consume the wrong amount, so resources are misallocated."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a private market may fail to provide the efficient quantity of vaccinations, which give benefits to people other than the person vaccinated.", "steps": [{"do": "Identify the failure: vaccination creates a positive externality of consumption.", "why": "Naming the correct type of market failure is the first step."}, {"do": "State that the individual considers only their own marginal private benefit (MPB).", "why": "Private decisions ignore benefits to third parties."}, {"do": "Explain that others gain lower infection risk, so marginal social benefit (MSB) is greater than MPB.", "why": "MSB = MPB + marginal external benefit."}, {"do": "Conclude that the free market quantity is where MPB meets marginal cost, below the social optimum where MSB meets it.", "why": "This shows under-consumption."}, {"do": "State the result: resources are under-allocated to vaccination and social welfare is not maximised.", "why": "This links back to the definition of market failure."}], "answer": "Vaccination has a positive externality. Buyers weigh only MPB, ignoring benefits to others, so MSB exceeds MPB. The market quantity, where MPB equals marginal cost, is below the socially optimal quantity. Resources are under-allocated, so welfare is not maximised."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer compare the free market quantity with the point where MSB meets marginal cost?", "o": ["The social optimum is where MSB equals marginal cost, so the gap shows the misallocation", "MSB is always the same as market demand", "Marginal cost is zero for vaccinations", "Firms always produce where MSB is highest"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: social efficiency is where marginal social benefit equals marginal cost; the shortfall from this shows market failure.", "MSB differs from private demand when externalities exist; that gap is the problem.", "Vaccinations have costs, so marginal cost is not zero.", "Firms follow private benefits and costs, not the highest MSB."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a chemical firm may over-produce when its factory pollutes a nearby river.", "given": ["The pollution is a negative externality of production.", "The firm considers only its marginal private cost (MPC)."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["Marginal social cost is greater than MPC because of the external cost to others", "Marginal social cost is below MPC because pollution lowers costs", "Marginal social benefit exceeds MPB because of the pollution", "The river is a public good, so the firm charges for it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: MSC = MPC + marginal external cost, so MSC lies above MPC.", "Pollution adds cost to society; MSC cannot be below MPC with a negative externality.", "This is a benefit-side statement; here the problem lies on the cost side.", "Charging is the issue only for public goods; the question concerns an externality."]}, "trap": "Do not just say a good is harmful. Show a gap between private and social costs or benefits, or a missing market, and say resources are misallocated.", "recap": "Market failure is misallocation of resources, caused by externalities, missing public goods or information gaps, so social welfare is not maximised."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Externality diagrams and explanations appear in Paper 1 and Paper 3 questions, and they underpin the case for government intervention.", "hook": {"q": "A factory's smoke harms nearby residents who never buy its products. Is that harm included in the factory's costs and so in the price?", "o": ["Yes, the market price always includes every cost", "No, the factory only counts its own costs", "Yes, but only if the residents are customers", "No, because smoke has no cost to anyone"], "a": 1, "f": ["Markets only include costs that the buyers and sellers themselves bear.", "Right. Private decision-makers count only their own costs, so harm to third parties is ignored.", "Whether the residents are customers is irrelevant; they are third parties either way.", "The smoke does have a cost, such as health damage; it is just not paid by the factory."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Private, external, social", "b": "**Private costs** (MPC) are paid by the producer; **private benefits** (MPB) go to the consumer. **External costs and benefits** fall on third parties. **Social cost** = private + external cost. **Social benefit** = private + external benefit."}, {"h": "Negative production externality", "b": "The firm ignores the **external cost**, so **MSC** lies above **MPC**. The gap is the external cost per unit. The market sets **MPB = MPC** at Qm, but the social optimum is where **MSB = MSC**, at a lower output, Q*."}, {"h": "Positive consumption externality", "b": "The consumer ignores the **external benefit**, so **MSB** lies above **MPB**. The market sets MPB = MPC at Qm. The social optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a higher output, Q*. The good is **under-consumed**."}, {"h": "Welfare loss and gain", "b": "The **welfare loss** is the triangle between MSC and MSB, between Q* and Qm, in the over-production case. Each unit there costs society more than it benefits. In the under-consumption case, the same triangle is the **welfare gain** from moving to Q*."}], "example": {"task": "Explain, with the help of a diagram, why a chemical works that pollutes a river leads to market failure.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the externality: the works pays its private costs, but the pollution imposes an external cost on third parties such as anglers, so MSC = MPC + external cost.", "why": "Defining the external cost shows why the MSC curve lies above MPC."}, {"do": "On the diagram, MSC is above MPC by the external cost per unit. The market equilibrium is where MPB = MPC, at output Qm.", "why": "Firms and consumers ignore the third-party cost."}, {"do": "The social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at Q*, which is to the left of Qm.", "why": "Social efficiency needs all costs and benefits to be counted."}, {"do": "Between Q* and Qm, MSC exceeds MSB, so the good is over-produced.", "why": "This shows the misallocation of resources."}, {"do": "The welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB from Q* to Qm.", "why": "It measures the net loss of social welfare."}], "answer": "The works ignores the external cost of pollution, so MSC lies above MPC. The market produces where MPB = MPC (Qm), beyond the social optimum where MSB = MSC (Q*). Over-production creates a welfare loss triangle, so there is market failure."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the welfare loss triangle only cover output between Q* and Qm?", "o": ["Because below Q* the external cost is zero", "Because the market does not produce below Q*", "Because below Q*, MSB exceeds MSC, so each unit adds net social benefit", "Because above Qm, firms stop producing"], "a": 2, "f": ["The external cost is present on every unit, not only beyond Q*.", "The market does produce those units; they are efficient, not a loss.", "Right. Up to Q*, MSB > MSC, so each unit adds net benefit. Only units beyond Q* cost society more than they give.", "Qm is where the market output stops because MPB = MPC, not because firms abruptly stop."]}, "faded": {"task": "Adult evening classes give an external benefit to employers and communities. Explain why the market under-provides them.", "given": ["Step 1: The learner ignores the external benefit, so MSB lies above MPB.", "Step 2: The market equilibrium is where MPB = MPC, at output Qm."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Show that the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at a higher output Q*, so classes are under-consumed", "Show that the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, at a lower output Q*, so classes are over-consumed", "Show that MSC lies above MPC, so the market over-produces", "Show that the welfare gain is the rectangle between Qm and Q*"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. MSB lies above MPB, so MSB = MSC is to the right of Qm, meaning under-consumption.", "With a positive externality the optimum is at a higher, not lower, output, so there is under-consumption.", "MSC above MPC is a negative production externality; this question is about a consumption benefit.", "The gain is a triangle between MSB and MSC, not a rectangle."]}, "trap": "Drawing welfare loss as a rectangle or the whole external cost. It is only the triangle between MSC and MSB from the social optimum to market output.", "recap": "Markets equate MPB with MPC, ignoring externalities; the social optimum is where MSB = MSC, and the gap between them is a welfare-loss triangle."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "Public goods are a standard market failure topic: expect definitions, examples and short explain questions on why markets under-provide them, often as 4 to 6 mark answers.", "hook": {"q": "A street has one lamp post. If you and your neighbour both use its light, does it get dimmer for either of you?", "o": ["No, and you can't stop the neighbour using it", "No, but the owner can easily stop the neighbour", "Yes, because it is shared between users", "Yes, but only at night"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Use doesn't reduce anyone else's, and nobody can be excluded. That's the core of a public good.", "Not for a lamp: you can't switch light off for one person, so exclusion is impractical.", "Light isn't used up by extra users, so it doesn't dim.", "Time of day doesn't change the point: more users don't reduce the light."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two defining features", "b": "A **pure public good** is **non-rival**: one person's consumption does not reduce the amount available to others. It is also **non-excludable**: people cannot be stopped from using it, even if they haven't paid. Examples: national defence, street lighting."}, {"h": "The free-rider problem", "b": "Because nobody can be excluded, people can enjoy the good without paying. This is the **free-rider problem**. Each person hopes others will pay. Firms cannot charge a price, so they earn too little revenue to cover costs."}, {"h": "Under-provision by markets", "b": "With no price signal, **effective demand** is far below true willingness to pay. Private firms will not supply, so there is a **missing market** or a very low output. This is market failure and means resources are misallocated."}, {"h": "Not the same as public provision", "b": "Public goods are defined by their **characteristics**, not by who provides them. The NHS and state schools are publicly provided but are rival or excludable. Governments often provide true public goods through **taxation**, which overcomes free riding."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a free market is unlikely to provide flood defences for a coastal town. (4 marks)", "steps": [{"do": "Flood defences are non-rival: protecting one household does not reduce the protection for others.", "why": "Defines the first characteristic and applies it to the context."}, {"do": "They are non-excludable: a sea wall protects every house behind it, whether or not the owner paid.", "why": "Defines the second characteristic with application."}, {"do": "So households can free ride, hoping neighbours pay, and a firm cannot charge those who benefit.", "why": "Links the characteristics to the free-rider problem."}, {"do": "Revenue falls short of cost, so no private firm supplies. The market is missing, causing resource misallocation.", "why": "Concludes with the consequence: under-provision as market failure."}], "answer": "Flood defences are non-rival and non-excludable. Households can free ride, so firms cannot charge everyone who benefits and revenue falls short of costs. No private firm will supply them. The market is missing, so resources are misallocated."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention that a firm cannot charge those who benefit?", "o": ["Non-excludability stops it collecting revenue, which is why the market fails", "Non-rivalry means the firm's costs are too high", "Flood defences are provided by the government by definition", "Consumers do not value flood defences"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Without the power to exclude, no price can be enforced, so revenue is inadequate.", "Non-rivalry means extra users cost nothing; the problem is revenue, not cost.", "Provider doesn't define the good; the characteristics do.", "People value protection highly; free riding hides it in the market."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why a free market may under-provide a national radio emergency-warning broadcast. (4 marks)", "given": ["The broadcast is non-rival: one listener tuning in does not reduce the signal for others.", "It is non-excludable: anyone with a radio can receive it without paying."], "q": "What is the next step in the answer?", "o": ["People can free ride, so no price can be charged and revenue is too low to cover costs", "The broadcast is a merit good, so people underestimate its benefits", "Government provision makes it a public good", "Marginal cost is high, so firms will not supply it"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. This links the two features to free riding and weak revenue, leading to under-provision.", "Merit goods involve information failure; this answer should follow the free-rider chain.", "Provision by government is a solution, not what makes it public, and it skips the explanation.", "Non-rivalry means the marginal cost of an extra listener is zero, not high."]}, "trap": "Don't call a good public just because the government provides it. Check both features: non-rival and non-excludable. Schools and the NHS fail that test.", "recap": "Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, so free riding prevents firms charging and markets under-provide them."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Information failure appears in multiple-choice, short answers and evaluation of government intervention across Papers 1 and 3.", "hook": {"q": "You buy a used phone online. The seller knows its battery is failing. You do not. What is likely to happen to the price you are willing to pay?", "o": ["You pay the price of a perfect phone", "You offer less, expecting some could be faulty", "You always refuse to buy any used phone", "The price is irrelevant to you"], "a": 1, "f": ["Without knowing quality you would not assume the best case.", "Right: uncertainty about quality lowers what buyers will pay, which is the start of market failure.", "Most buyers still buy, just at a lower price for the risk.", "Price is how buyers respond to the risk."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Symmetric and asymmetric", "b": "**Symmetric information** means buyers and sellers have the same information. **Asymmetric information** means one party knows more than the other. Sellers often know more (used cars), but buyers can know more too (insurance applicants)."}, {"h": "Why resources are misallocated", "b": "Decisions rely on price signals and perceived costs and benefits. If these are wrong, consumers buy too much or too little. This causes **misallocation of resources** and a loss of **allocative efficiency**: a form of **market failure**."}, {"h": "Examples of gaps", "b": "**Adverse selection**: hidden information before a deal, such as risky people buying insurance. **Moral hazard**: riskier behaviour after the deal, such as being less careful once insured. Consumers may also misjudge harm from **demerit goods** or benefits of **merit goods**."}, {"h": "Possible remedies", "b": "Government can require **labelling**, run **information campaigns**, or regulate professionals. Firms can offer **guarantees** and ratings. Limits: **cost**, consumers may ignore information, and **government failure** from poor data."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how asymmetric information in the market for second-hand cars can lead to market failure.", "steps": [{"do": "Sellers know the true quality of a car; buyers cannot tell good cars from poor ones.", "why": "Defines the asymmetric information precisely and names who is better informed."}, {"do": "Buyers are unsure, so they offer a price reflecting average quality.", "why": "Links the information gap to the price signal."}, {"do": "Owners of good cars find the price too low and withdraw them, leaving mainly poor cars.", "why": "Shows adverse selection: the better products leave the market."}, {"do": "The market shrinks and some mutually beneficial trades do not happen.", "why": "Shows resources are not allocated where they are valued most."}, {"do": "So the market fails: output differs from the allocatively efficient level.", "why": "Concludes with the link to market failure."}], "answer": "Sellers know car quality; buyers do not. Buyers offer an average price, so good-car owners withdraw, leaving poor cars. Fewer beneficial trades occur, so resources are misallocated and the market fails."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say good cars are withdrawn from the market?", "o": ["Their owners receive a price below the value of their car", "Government bans them from sale", "Buyers prefer faulty cars", "Good cars are cheaper to produce"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: the average price undervalues good cars, so their sellers exit.", "Nothing here involves a ban; the cause is the price signal.", "Buyers do not prefer faulty cars; they simply cannot tell the difference.", "Production cost is irrelevant to this second-hand sale."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how moral hazard in car insurance can cause market failure.", "given": ["Once a driver is insured, the insurer bears much of the cost of any accident.", "The insurer cannot observe how carefully the driver behaves."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The driver may take more risks, so more claims occur than the insurer expected", "Drivers with high risk buy insurance before the contract", "The government sets a price ceiling on premiums", "Premiums fall because accidents are fewer"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: behaviour changes after the contract, increasing risk and costs.", "That describes adverse selection, which happens before the contract.", "A price ceiling is a policy, not the next step of the explanation.", "Riskier behaviour raises claims, so premiums would not fall."]}, "trap": "Do not say asymmetric information only hurts buyers, or confuse adverse selection (before the deal) with moral hazard (after it). Always finish by linking to misallocation.", "recap": "Asymmetric information distorts decisions and price signals, so resources are misallocated and the market fails."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "Government intervention appears in Paper 1 and Paper 3 as diagram-based explain and evaluate questions; accurate tax, subsidy and price-control effects underpin your analysis marks.", "hook": {"q": "A government taxes cigarettes heavily. Smokers find it hard to cut down. Who is likely to bear most of the tax?", "o": ["Smokers, because their demand is inelastic", "Tobacco firms, because they pay the tax to the government", "Both equally, because tax is always split 50:50", "Nobody, because the price stays the same"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Inelastic demand means buyers keep buying, so firms pass most of the tax on in the price.", "Firms hand over the tax, but who bears it depends on elasticity, not on who pays the government.", "The split depends on PED and PES, not a fixed 50:50.", "The tax raises costs, so price rises; it does not stay the same."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Taxes and subsidies", "b": "A **specific tax** is a fixed £ per unit and shifts supply left in parallel. An **ad valorem tax** is a percentage of price, so the supply curve pivots. A **subsidy** lowers costs and shifts supply right. Both change equilibrium price and quantity."}, {"h": "Who bears the tax", "b": "**Tax incidence** is the share of the tax borne by consumers and producers. The less elastic side bears more. If demand is less elastic than supply, consumers pay most; if supply is less elastic than demand, producers pay most. Consumer burden is the price rise, not the full tax."}, {"h": "Price controls", "b": "A **maximum price** set below equilibrium causes **excess demand** (a shortage). A **minimum price** set above equilibrium causes **excess supply** (a surplus). A maximum price set above equilibrium, or a minimum price set below it, has no effect, because it is not binding."}, {"h": "Other methods", "b": "**Tradable permits** cap pollution and let firms trade the right to emit. **State provision** means government supplies the good directly. **Regulation** sets legal rules, and **information provision** corrects imperfect information. Each carries costs and possible government failure."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a subsidy on bus travel affects the market and who benefits.", "steps": [{"do": "A per-unit subsidy lowers firms' costs of supplying, so the supply curve shifts right (down) by the amount of the subsidy.", "why": "Subsidies change supply, not demand."}, {"do": "The new equilibrium has a lower price and a higher quantity, because the right shift moves along the demand curve.", "why": "Shows the market outcome."}, {"do": "Consumers gain through a lower price, but the price falls by less than the subsidy unless supply is perfectly elastic or demand is perfectly inelastic.", "why": "Incidence is shared."}, {"do": "Producers also gain, since the price they receive is the consumer price plus the subsidy.", "why": "Both sides benefit."}, {"do": "The government pays the subsidy cost, an opportunity cost that could fund other spending.", "why": "Links to a cost of intervention."}], "answer": "Supply shifts right by the subsidy, lowering price and raising quantity. Consumers and producers share the gain by elasticity. The government bears the cost, with an opportunity cost."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say the consumers' price fall is less than the subsidy?", "o": ["Because the subsidy is shared between consumers and producers", "Because demand shifts left when subsidies are paid", "Because subsidies only help producers", "Because the government keeps part of the subsidy"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Producers receive part of the subsidy as a higher price received, so consumers see a smaller fall.", "Subsidies do not shift demand; they shift supply.", "Both sides gain; consumers get a lower price.", "The government pays out the full subsidy; it does not keep part."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain the effect of a £2 specific tax on a good whose supply is price inelastic and less elastic than demand.", "given": ["The tax shifts supply left (up) by £2 at every quantity."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Producers bear most of the tax, as they cannot cut supply easily, so price rises by less than £2", "Consumers bear the full £2 because the tax shifts supply", "Price falls because supply has fallen", "Quantity traded rises as firms get higher revenue"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Supply is the less elastic side, so firms bear most of the tax and the consumer price rises by less than £2.", "Consumers bear the full £2 only if demand is perfectly inelastic or supply perfectly elastic; here supply is the less elastic side.", "Lower supply raises the equilibrium price; it does not lower it.", "A leftward shift reduces equilibrium quantity, not raises it."]}, "trap": "Do not assume consumers pay the whole tax. The consumer share is the price rise, and the rest falls on producers; check which side is less elastic.", "recap": "Taxes and subsidies shift supply, and the burden or benefit is shared according to relative price elasticities."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Evaluation questions on intervention often ask you to judge a policy, and government failure is a main counter-argument in extended answers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What counts as failure", "b": "**Government failure** occurs when intervention causes a **net welfare loss**, so resources are allocated less efficiently than without it. A policy that only misses its target is not enough. You must show the outcome is worse overall."}, {"h": "Main causes", "b": "Key causes: **unintended consequences** (such as black markets), **distortion of price signals**, **administrative costs**, and **information gaps** (the government cannot know the right tax or price)."}, {"h": "Showing it with diagrams", "b": "A tax set above the marginal external cost shifts supply too far left. Output falls below the social optimum. The **welfare loss** is the triangle between marginal social benefit and marginal social cost on the lost units."}, {"h": "Evaluating", "b": "Weigh government failure against **market failure**. Judge by size: is the welfare loss smaller than the original one? Consider time, data quality and the scale of the policy. Reach a supported conclusion."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate whether a government price cap on energy bills is likely to lead to government failure. (Model answer built in parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "A price cap sets a maximum price below the free market equilibrium, so quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls.", "why": "Knowledge: defines the intervention and its direct effect on the market."}, {"do": "In the energy market, if suppliers cannot cover costs they may cut investment or leave, so supply shrinks and shortages emerge, distorting price signals.", "why": "Application and analysis: links the policy to a causal chain and a specific cause of failure."}, {"do": "This creates a welfare loss, because units valued above their cost are no longer produced, and administration of the cap adds costs, so the outcome may be worse than no intervention.", "why": "Analysis: shows the net welfare loss, which defines government failure."}, {"do": "However, if the cap is set close to the equilibrium, the loss may be small compared with the gain to vulnerable households, so judgement depends on its level and duration.", "why": "Evaluation: weighs magnitude and time, balancing the argument."}, {"do": "Overall, failure is likely if the cap is long-term and set well below cost, but a modest, temporary cap may bring net benefits.", "why": "Conclusion: a supported judgement that follows from the reasoning."}], "answer": "A cap below equilibrium creates excess demand, can cut supply and investment, and distorts price signals, causing a welfare loss and admin costs. Failure is likely if long-term and far below cost, but a modest, temporary cap may bring net benefits."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the model answer end with a judgement about the level and duration of the cap?", "o": ["Because evaluation needs a supported conclusion based on magnitude and time", "Because the cap level never affects the welfare loss", "Because the answer must always conclude failure occurs", "Because definitions earn the marks at the end of an essay"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: evaluation marks reward weighing factors such as size and time and reaching a justified conclusion.", "Wrong: the level of the cap strongly affects the size of the welfare loss.", "Wrong: conclusions must follow the reasoning, and failure is not always the answer.", "Wrong: definitions earn knowledge marks early on; the end is for judgement."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate whether a government subsidy for electric cars may cause government failure.", "given": ["Knowledge: A subsidy lowers costs, shifting supply right so price falls and output rises.", "Application: Take-up may be mostly by richer households who would have bought anyway."], "q": "What is the best next step in the answer?", "o": ["Explain that the cost to taxpayers and wasted spending may outweigh the benefit, causing a net welfare loss", "State that subsidies always remove externalities so there can be no failure", "Define market failure at length without linking to the subsidy", "Conclude immediately that the subsidy has succeeded because output rose"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: it develops analysis of opportunity cost and a net welfare loss, the core of government failure.", "Wrong: assuming a subsidy always works ignores possible over-subsidy and wasted spending.", "Wrong: a long definition is not linked to the case, so it earns little analysis credit.", "Wrong: more output alone does not show net welfare gain; costs must be weighed."]}, "trap": "Do not say a policy is government failure just because it missed its target. Show a net welfare loss compared with no intervention, then weigh it against the original market failure.", "recap": "Government failure is intervention causing a net welfare loss; evaluate by weighing its size against the market failure it aimed to correct."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.1", "kind": "method", "why": "Theme 2 is examined on Papers 2 and 3, where you may need to calculate real GDP and GDP per head before judging living standards.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Real versus nominal GDP", "b": "**Nominal GDP** values output at current prices, so inflation inflates the figure. **Real GDP** uses constant prices to show volume growth: $\\text{Real GDP}=\\frac{\\text{Nominal GDP}}{\\text{Price index}}\\times 100$. Always use real figures to measure economic growth."}, {"h": "GDP per head and GNI", "b": "Divide real GDP by population to get **real GDP per head**, showing average output per person. **Gross National Income** (GNI) adds net property income from abroad to GDP: income UK residents earn on assets overseas minus income foreign owners earn on UK assets."}, {"h": "PPPs and wellbeing limits", "b": "**Purchasing Power Parities** (PPPs) adjust exchange rates for local living costs when comparing countries. Even PPP real GDP per head ignores **income distribution**, unpaid work, pollution and leisure, so economists also track ONS **national wellbeing** measures."}], "example": {"task": "A made-up economy has Year 1 nominal GDP £200bn (price index 100, population 10m) and Year 2 nominal GDP £231bn (price index 105, population 11m). Find real GDP growth and real GDP per head growth.", "steps": [{"do": "Year 1 real GDP is $£200\\text{bn}$ because the base index is 100. Year 1 real GDP per head is $\\frac{£200\\text{bn}}{10\\text{m}}=£20,000$.", "why": "At a base index of 100, nominal and real values are equal."}, {"do": "Convert Year 2 nominal GDP to real GDP: $\\frac{£231\\text{bn}}{105}\\times 100=£220\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Dividing by the price index removes the 5% rise in prices."}, {"do": "Calculate total real GDP growth: $\\frac{220-200}{200}\\times 100=10\\%$.", "why": "Percentage change equals new minus old, divided by old, times 100."}, {"do": "Find Year 2 real GDP per head: $\\frac{£220\\text{bn}}{11\\text{m}}=£20,000$, so growth per head is $0\\%$.", "why": "Population also rose by 10%, leaving average real output per person unchanged."}], "answer": "Total real GDP grew by 10%, from £200bn to £220bn, but real GDP per head stayed at £20,000 (0% growth) because population grew at the same 10% rate."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why did Step 2 divide £231bn by 105 and multiply by 100 instead of comparing £231bn directly with £200bn?", "o": ["To convert the figure from GDP into Gross National Income", "To remove the 5% inflation included in Year 2 nominal GDP", "To adjust the total figure for the 1m rise in population", "To express the output using Purchasing Power Parity rates"], "a": 1, "f": ["Converting GDP to GNI adds net property income from abroad, not a price index.", "Deflating by the price index removes price rises so only volume growth remains.", "Population is accounted for in Step 4 by dividing real GDP by population.", "PPPs adjust for cross-country price differences, not inflation between two years in one country."]}, "faded": {"task": "In another made-up economy, Year 1 real GDP is £400bn (index 100). In Year 2, nominal GDP is £462bn and the price index is 110. Find percentage real GDP growth.", "given": ["Year 1 real GDP = $£400\\text{bn}$.", "Year 2 real GDP = $\\frac{£462\\text{bn}}{110}\\times 100 = £420\\text{bn}$."], "q": "What is the next step to find percentage real GDP growth?", "o": ["$\\frac{462-400}{400}\\times 100 = 15.5\\%$", "$\\frac{420-400}{420}\\times 100 = 4.8\\%$", "$\\frac{420-400}{400}\\times 100 = 5.0\\%$", "$\\frac{462-420}{420}\\times 100 = 10.0\\%$"], "a": 2, "f": ["This uses Year 2 nominal GDP (£462bn) instead of deflated real GDP (£420bn).", "This divides by the new value (£420bn) instead of the original Year 1 value (£400bn).", "Subtracting old real GDP from new real GDP and dividing by old real GDP gives 5.0%.", "This calculates the 10% inflation rate between nominal and real GDP, not real growth."]}, "trap": "Students often compute nominal growth or divide by the new figure instead of the base figure. Always deflate first, divide by Year 1, then check population.", "recap": "Always deflate nominal GDP with the price index before calculating growth, and divide by population to judge living standards."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Inflation questions appear in Papers 1 and 2 as data and short answers, and underpin macro evaluation. You must define terms precisely and explain causes and effects.", "hook": {"q": "The inflation rate falls from 5% to 3%. What is happening to prices in the shops?", "o": ["They are falling on average", "They are still rising, but more slowly", "They are unchanged", "They have fallen by 2%"], "a": 1, "f": ["Falling prices would need a negative inflation rate, not 3%.", "Right. A positive rate means the general price level is still rising, just at a slower pace.", "Unchanged prices would mean 0% inflation.", "The 2 points is the fall in the rate, not a fall in prices."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three key terms", "b": "**Inflation** is a sustained rise in the general price level. **Deflation** is a sustained fall in the general price level, so the rate is negative. **Disinflation** is a fall in the rate of inflation: prices still rise, more slowly."}, {"h": "Measuring inflation", "b": "The **CPI** tracks the cost of a **weighted basket** of goods and services bought by households, with weights based on spending shares. The **RPI** also includes housing costs such as mortgage interest and council tax. Rate = change in index ÷ old index × 100."}, {"h": "Causes of inflation", "b": "**Demand-pull** inflation comes from AD rising faster than the economy's capacity, shifting AD right. **Cost-push** inflation comes from higher production costs, shifting SRAS left. Rapid growth in the **money supply** can also fuel demand-pull inflation."}, {"h": "Effects of inflation", "b": "Inflation erodes **purchasing power** and the **real value** of savings if interest rates are below inflation. Firms face **menu costs** and uncertainty, which can reduce investment. Higher UK prices can cut **international competitiveness**, so exports fall."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a rise in aggregate demand can cause inflation.", "steps": [{"do": "AD rises, for example after a cut in interest rates raises consumption and investment. The AD curve shifts right.", "why": "Names a specific trigger and the diagram movement."}, {"do": "If the economy is near full capacity, the SRAS curve is steep, so extra demand raises prices more than output.", "why": "Links the size of the price effect to spare capacity."}, {"do": "Firms compete for scarce labour and materials, so their costs rise and they pass these on in higher prices.", "why": "Shows the mechanism behind the price rise."}, {"do": "The general price level rises. If this continues over time, it is demand-pull inflation.", "why": "Concludes by tying to the definition of sustained rise."}], "answer": "A cut in interest rates raises consumption and investment, shifting AD right. Near full capacity, SRAS is steep, so output rises little. Firms bid up wages and input costs and raise prices. The general price level rises; if sustained, this is demand-pull inflation."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say the economy is near full capacity?", "o": ["Because AD can only shift right at full capacity", "Because with little spare capacity, extra demand mainly raises prices, not output", "Because full capacity means SRAS shifts left", "Because inflation cannot occur below full capacity"], "a": 1, "f": ["AD can shift right at any level of output; capacity affects the result, not the shift.", "Right. With little spare capacity SRAS is steep, so more demand pushes up prices rather than output.", "A leftward SRAS shift is cost-push; here SRAS does not move.", "Prices can rise below full capacity, though by less; it is a matter of degree."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain one effect of inflation on savers.", "given": ["Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level.", "Suppose a savings account pays 2% interest and inflation is 4%."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["The real interest rate is about −2%, so the purchasing power of savings falls", "The real interest rate is +6%, so savers gain purchasing power", "The real interest rate is 2%, because inflation does not affect interest", "The real interest rate is about −2%, so savers gain purchasing power"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Real rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation = 2% − 4% = −2%, so savings buy less than before.", "6% adds inflation instead of subtracting it.", "Inflation affects the real return, even though the nominal rate stays at 2%.", "The calculation is right, but a negative real rate means savers lose purchasing power."]}, "trap": "Calling any fall in the inflation rate 'deflation'. Check the sign: a positive rate that falls is disinflation; only a negative rate is deflation.", "recap": "Inflation is a sustained rise in prices; disinflation is slower inflation; deflation is falling prices; CPI measures it with a weighted basket."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.3", "kind": "idea", "why": "The 9EC0 specification asks you to distinguish the ILO (Labour Force Survey) and Claimant Count measures and to explain the types and consequences of unemployment.", "hook": {"q": "An unemployed parent stops applying for jobs to care full-time for a relative. What happens to the UK ILO unemployment figure?", "o": ["It rises by one because they still have no job", "It stays the same until they reach pension age", "It falls by one as they become economically inactive", "It falls only if they were claiming benefits"], "a": 2, "f": ["To count as ILO unemployed you must be actively seeking work and available to start.", "Age does not matter: someone neither seeking work nor available to start is economically inactive.", "Stopping job search moves a person from unemployed to economically inactive, reducing the ILO count.", "Benefit status affects the Claimant Count, not the ILO survey definition."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Two UK unemployment measures", "b": "The **Labour Force Survey** (ILO measure) counts jobless people who actively sought work in the past four weeks and can start within two weeks. The **Claimant Count** records people claiming unemployment-related benefits. People neither working nor seeking work are **economically inactive**."}, {"h": "Main types of unemployment", "b": "**Cyclical unemployment** comes from a lack of aggregate demand in a downturn. **Structural unemployment** arises when industries decline and workers face occupational or geographical immobility. **Frictional unemployment** is short-term search between jobs; seasonal and real-wage unemployment are the other main types."}, {"h": "Effects on the economy", "b": "High unemployment cuts household incomes and firm sales, and worsens the **fiscal balance** through lower tax receipts and higher welfare spending. Long-term joblessness causes **hysteresis**, where lost skills reduce future employability. Rising **underemployment** also leaves labour resources underused."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the UK Labour Force Survey (ILO) measure of unemployment can differ from the Claimant Count.", "steps": [{"do": "Define both measures: the LFS counts people without a job who have actively sought work and are available to start, whereas the Claimant Count counts people claiming unemployment-related benefits.", "why": "Clear definitions show that the two measures count different groups."}, {"do": "Point out that some jobseekers are not eligible for these benefits, for example because of a partner's income or their own savings.", "why": "Benefit rules can leave genuine jobseekers out of the Claimant Count while the LFS still counts them."}, {"do": "Note that some eligible jobseekers choose not to claim because of stigma or the effort of applying, yet they still appear in the LFS.", "why": "Non-take-up is a second reason the LFS can exceed the Claimant Count."}, {"do": "Note that under Universal Credit some people in low-paid work must search for more work, so they enter the Claimant Count although the LFS counts them as employed.", "why": "This pushes the Claimant Count up, so the gap can run either way."}, {"do": "Conclude that eligibility rules and non-take-up push the LFS above the Claimant Count, while Universal Credit rules push the Claimant Count up, so neither measure is always the larger.", "why": "Linking back to the question completes a coherent explanation."}], "answer": "The two measures differ because the LFS counts all jobless people actively seeking and available for work, whereas the Claimant Count leaves out jobseekers who are ineligible or do not claim, but includes some low-paid workers on Universal Credit who must look for more work. So either figure can be the higher one."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 2 mention jobseekers with a higher-earning partner or savings?", "o": ["Because they are classed as economically inactive in the LFS", "Because they are counted twice in the Labour Force Survey", "Because they always count as structurally unemployed workers", "Because benefit rules can exclude them from the Claimant Count despite job search"], "a": 3, "f": ["If they actively seek work and are available, the LFS counts them as unemployed.", "The LFS is a household sample survey and does not double-count respondents.", "Ineligibility for benefits does not determine which type of unemployment a worker faces.", "Means-tested rules can keep them out of the Claimant Count, while the LFS still counts them if they seek work."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a UK recession affects a small high-street toy shop and its staff.", "given": ["A recession lowers aggregate demand, causing cyclical unemployment across the economy.", "With lower household incomes, fewer customers buy non-essential gifts at the toy shop."], "q": "Which next linked point best develops the economic effects on the firm and workers?", "o": ["Lower sales force cuts in hours or staff, raising underemployment or unemployment", "Lower sales raise the shop's profits because its tax rate automatically drops to zero", "Staff who lose hours immediately leave the economy and count as seasonal workers", "Reduced demand shifts the labour supply curve rightwards and raises hourly wages"], "a": 0, "f": ["This links lower firm revenue directly to labour demand, underemployment and job losses.", "Lower sales usually cut profits, and tax rates do not automatically fall to zero in a recession.", "Cutting hours creates underemployment, and losing a job in a slump is cyclical, not seasonal.", "Lower demand for toys shifts the derived demand for retail labour leftwards, not supply rightwards."]}, "trap": "Students often call everyone without a job unemployed. Check whether they are actively seeking work and available; if not, they are economically inactive.", "recap": "Unemployment excludes the economically inactive, differs between LFS and Claimant Count, and depends on demand, structure and skills."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.1.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Theme 2 is examined on Papers 2 and 3, where you may be given balance of payments figures to calculate and interpret the current account balance.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Trade in goods and services", "b": "The balance of payments records financial flows between the UK and the rest of the world. **Trade in goods** is physical exports minus imports, while **trade in services** covers invisibles such as banking, insurance and tourism. Together they form the **trade balance**."}, {"h": "Primary and secondary income", "b": "**Primary income** records net flows of wages, interest, profits and dividends earned on cross-border investments. **Secondary income** records net current transfers where nothing is supplied in return, such as overseas aid and remittances."}, {"h": "Deficits and interconnectedness", "b": "A **current account deficit** means total outflows across all four parts exceed inflows. It is financed by net inflows on the **financial account**. Interconnectedness means deficits in some countries are matched by surpluses in others, and a slowdown abroad cuts demand for UK exports."}], "example": {"task": "A made-up economy has goods exports £180bn, goods imports £240bn, services exports £110bn, services imports £75bn, net primary income −£15bn and net secondary income −£10bn. Find its current account balance.", "steps": [{"do": "Calculate trade in goods: $£180\\text{bn}-£240\\text{bn}=-£60\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Exports are credits (+) and imports are debits (−)."}, {"do": "Calculate trade in services: $£110\\text{bn}-£75\\text{bn}=+£35\\text{bn}$.", "why": "Service exports exceed service imports, creating a services surplus."}, {"do": "Find the overall trade balance: $-£60\\text{bn}+£35\\text{bn}=-£25\\text{bn}$.", "why": "The services surplus partly offsets the goods deficit."}, {"do": "Add primary and secondary income: $-£25\\text{bn}+(-£15\\text{bn})+(-£10\\text{bn})=-£50\\text{bn}$.", "why": "The current account includes all four components, giving a £50bn deficit."}], "answer": "The economy has a trade deficit of £25bn and an overall current account deficit of £50bn (−£50bn)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 add −£15bn and −£10bn after already finding the −£25bn trade balance?", "o": ["Because primary and secondary income are part of the current account", "Because every deficit must be doubled to match the financial account", "Because goods exports are omitted until the final step", "Because overseas aid is subtracted twice under Edexcel rules"], "a": 0, "f": ["The current account equals trade in goods, trade in services, primary income and secondary income combined.", "The financial account finances the deficit separately; you never double a trade deficit.", "Goods exports were already included in Step 1 when finding the goods balance.", "Overseas aid is included once inside secondary income, never subtracted twice."]}, "faded": {"task": "Another made-up economy has trade in goods −£40bn, trade in services +£52bn, net primary income −£8bn and net secondary income −£6bn. Find its current account balance.", "given": ["Trade balance = $-£40\\text{bn} + £52\\text{bn} = +£12\\text{bn}$."], "q": "What is the next step to find the overall current account balance?", "o": ["$+£12\\text{bn} + £8\\text{bn} + £6\\text{bn} = +£26\\text{bn}$ surplus", "$+£12\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = -£2\\text{bn}$ deficit", "$-£40\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = -£54\\text{bn}$ deficit", "$+£52\\text{bn} - £8\\text{bn} - £6\\text{bn} = +£38\\text{bn}$ surplus"], "a": 1, "f": ["This flips the negative signs on primary and secondary income, turning net outflows into inflows.", "Adding −£8bn and −£6bn to the +£12bn trade surplus leaves a −£2bn current account deficit.", "This forgets the +£52bn surplus on trade in services.", "This omits the −£40bn deficit on trade in goods."]}, "trap": "Students often stop at exports minus imports and forget primary and secondary income, or misread minus signs. Always list all four components with clear + and − signs.", "recap": "Sum trade in goods, trade in services, primary income and secondary income with careful signs to find the current account balance."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.1", "kind": "idea", "why": "AD/AS analysis runs through Edexcel Theme 2, so you must separate movements along the AD curve from shifts of it accurately.", "hook": {"q": "If the UK's general price level falls while government policy and confidence stay the same, what happens to the AD curve?", "o": ["The whole AD curve shifts to the right", "There is a movement down along the existing AD curve", "The whole AD curve shifts to the left", "The AD curve rotates to become horizontal"], "a": 1, "f": ["A rightward shift needs a non-price rise in C, I, G or (X−M) at every price level.", "Price level is on the vertical axis, so a lower price level causes an extension along AD.", "Lower prices raise real quantity of output demanded rather than shifting AD left.", "The AD curve remains downward-sloping; a price change moves along it rather than flattening it."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Components of aggregate demand", "b": "**Aggregate demand** (AD) is total planned spending on UK-produced goods and services at a given price level: $AD=C+I+G+(X-M)$. **Net trade** $(X-M)$ subtracts imports because spending on foreign goods leaves the UK circular flow."}, {"h": "Relative size in the UK", "b": "In the UK, **consumption** ($C$) is the largest component of AD, so even a small percentage change in household spending is a large change in AD in pounds. **Government expenditure** ($G$) is next largest, **investment** ($I$) is smaller but volatile, and net trade is usually small and negative."}, {"h": "Why AD slopes downwards", "b": "A lower price level increases real output demanded through three channels: the **wealth effect** (savings buy more), the **trade effect** (UK exports look cheaper abroad), and the **interest rate effect** (less money is needed for transactions, so interest rates tend to fall and borrowing rises)."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why the aggregate demand curve slopes downwards from left to right.", "steps": [{"do": "State what the axes show: the general price level on the vertical axis and real GDP (output) on the horizontal axis, with an inverse relationship.", "why": "Naming the axes grounds the explanation in the Edexcel AD diagram."}, {"do": "Explain the trade effect: if the UK price level falls relative to other countries, UK exports become more price-competitive and imports look dearer, raising $(X-M)$.", "why": "Higher net trade directly increases the real output demanded at lower prices."}, {"do": "Explain the wealth effect: a lower price level raises the real purchasing power of households' cash and savings, encouraging higher consumption ($C$).", "why": "Higher real wealth boosts household spending along the AD curve."}, {"do": "Explain the interest rate effect: lower prices reduce the demand for money and inflation pressure, allowing lower interest rates that encourage borrowing for $C$ and $I$.", "why": "Lower interest rates make borrowing cheaper, so spending rises as the price level falls."}], "answer": "AD slopes downwards because a lower price level makes UK exports more competitive (raising $X-M$), increases the real value of household wealth (raising $C$), and allows lower interest rates (boosting $C$ and $I$), so higher real output is demanded."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 2 say UK exports become more price-competitive when the UK price level falls?", "o": ["Because the UK government automatically pays export subsidies", "Because a lower price level eliminates all UK imports", "Because UK goods become cheaper relative to foreign rivals", "Because real GDP is always fixed when prices change"], "a": 2, "f": ["The trade effect works through relative prices, not government subsidies.", "Lower UK prices reduce demand for some imports, but never eliminate imports completely.", "Lower UK prices make UK products better value abroad, increasing export demand and cutting imports.", "Moving down along AD increases real output demanded; real GDP is not fixed."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how a cut in UK income tax shifts the aggregate demand curve.", "given": ["A cut in income tax leaves UK households with higher disposable income at every price level.", "Households spend a large share of this extra income, so consumption ($C$) rises."], "q": "Which next linked point completes the explanation using the AD diagram?", "o": ["Because taxes fell, there is a downward movement along the existing AD curve", "The AD curve shifts leftwards because government tax revenue has fallen", "Only the trade balance changes, leaving the AD curve in the same position", "Higher $C$ at every price level shifts the whole AD curve to the right"], "a": 3, "f": ["Tax cuts are a non-price factor, so they shift the whole AD curve rather than moving along it.", "Lower tax receipts affect the budget balance, while higher household spending shifts AD rightwards.", "Income tax cuts act primarily through household consumption (C), directly shifting AD.", "A rise in C not caused by the price level is a non-price change, so AD shifts rightwards."]}, "trap": "Students often shift AD rightwards when the price level falls. Remember: price level is on the axis, so price changes move along AD; only changes in C, I, G or (X−M) shift it.", "recap": "Price-level changes cause movements along AD, whereas independent changes in $C$, $I$, $G$ or $(X-M)$ shift the AD curve."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel Paper 2 questions can ask you to analyse and evaluate how interest rates or wealth affect UK consumption, and the higher-mark parts reward a supported judgement.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Disposable income and MPC", "b": "The main driver of consumption is **disposable income** (gross income minus direct taxes plus cash benefits). The **marginal propensity to consume** ($MPC=\\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta Y}$) shows the share of extra income spent. The **saving ratio** is the percentage of disposable income saved."}, {"h": "Interest rates and credit", "b": "Higher **interest rates** raise the **cost of borrowing** on credit cards, loans and mortgages, while making saving more rewarding. Rising mortgage repayments cut households' **discretionary income**, reducing spending on non-essential goods and shifting AD leftwards."}, {"h": "Wealth and consumer confidence", "b": "Rising house or share prices create a positive **wealth effect**, encouraging households to spend more or borrow against home equity. Low **consumer confidence**, by contrast, triggers **precautionary saving** as households prepare for possible job losses."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the likely effect of a rise in UK Bank Rate from 3% to 5% on UK household consumption.", "steps": [{"do": "Consumption is household spending on goods and services, and a rise in Bank Rate normally pushes up mortgage, loan and savings rates across UK banks.", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining key terms and linking Bank Rate to retail rates."}, {"do": "A 2 percentage point rise, from 3% to 5%, raises variable-rate mortgage repayments and cuts households' discretionary income, while dearer credit and higher savings returns raise the opportunity cost of spending, reducing $C$ and shifting AD leftwards.", "why": "Earns Application marks by using the figures in the question, and Analysis marks through a clear chain of reasoning on borrowing and saving."}, {"do": "Higher mortgage costs also cool UK house price growth, creating a negative wealth effect that makes homeowners feel less well off and delay big-ticket purchases.", "why": "Adds a second analytical channel (wealth effect) needed for top-level Edexcel analysis."}, {"do": "However, the impact may be slow or limited because many UK homeowners hold fixed-rate mortgages, net savers gain extra interest income, and strong consumer confidence could keep spending high.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by weighing timing lags and counter-effects on different household groups."}, {"do": "On balance, consumption is likely to fall, but gradually rather than at once, because the hit grows as fixed-rate deals expire; how far it falls depends mainly on how indebted households are and how confident they remain.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on which effects dominate and what the outcome depends on."}], "answer": "A rise in Bank Rate from 3% to 5% is likely to cut UK consumption through dearer credit, lower discretionary income for borrowers and a weaker housing wealth effect, shifting AD left. Yet fixed-rate mortgages delay the hit, net savers gain income, and high confidence may soften the fall. On balance consumption falls gradually, by an amount that depends on household debt levels and confidence."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 bring in fixed-rate mortgages and net savers?", "o": ["To prove that higher interest rates always increase total UK consumption", "To define the formula for the marginal propensity to consume", "To show that interest rates only affect firms and never households", "To evaluate why the fall in consumption might be delayed or partly offset"], "a": 3, "f": ["Net savers' gains usually only dampen the fall; they do not prove total consumption rises.", "Step 4 is evaluating the size of the effect, not giving the MPC definition.", "Step 2 already showed how higher rates directly affect household borrowing and saving.", "Edexcel evaluation rewards showing why an effect depends on timing lags and uneven impacts across groups."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a sustained rise in UK house prices affects household consumption.", "given": ["Higher house prices increase homeowners' housing wealth, creating a positive wealth effect.", "Homeowners feel more financially secure and may borrow against rising equity, boosting consumption and shifting AD rightwards."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["Therefore, house prices are always the same thing as monthly disposable income", "However, would-be buyers need bigger deposits, and many owners treat gains as paper wealth", "In addition, higher house prices mean every UK household stops saving altogether", "Consequently, higher house prices reduce consumption by cutting the MPC to zero"], "a": 1, "f": ["Housing wealth is a stock, not an income flow like disposable income, so this contains a conceptual error.", "This weighs losers (would-be first-time buyers) against winners and questions whether owners spend unrealised gains, qualifying the wealth effect.", "Rising house prices may lower the saving ratio, but it is false that every household stops saving, and the sentence does not weigh the argument.", "A positive wealth effect raises rather than cuts consumption and never pushes MPC to zero."]}, "trap": "Students often treat wealth and income as identical, or forget to evaluate who gains and loses. Distinguish flows from stocks, and always weigh lags and distributional effects.", "recap": "Consumption is driven by disposable income, interest rates, confidence and wealth, with its size shaped by MPC and household circumstances."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.3", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel essays regularly ask you to evaluate how interest rates, business confidence or corporation tax affect UK business investment.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Gross and net investment", "b": "**Gross investment** is total spending by firms on new capital goods before accounting for wear and tear (**depreciation**). **Net investment** equals gross investment minus depreciation. Only positive net investment increases the capital stock and long-run capacity."}, {"h": "Growth and animal spirits", "b": "A higher **rate of economic growth** boosts expected sales and encourages firms to expand capacity. Keynes stressed **animal spirits**: business confidence and instincts under uncertainty. Higher **retained profit** gives firms internal funds for projects."}, {"h": "Interest rates, credit and tax", "b": "Higher interest rates raise borrowing costs and the **opportunity cost** of using retained profits instead of earning interest. Poor **access to credit** blocks loans regardless of rates, while lower corporation tax leaves firms more post-tax profit to invest."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate whether a cut in UK interest rates will significantly increase business investment.", "steps": [{"do": "Investment is firms' spending on capital goods. Lower interest rates cut the cost of bank loans and reduce the opportunity cost of using retained profits instead of saving them.", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining investment and identifying both interest-rate channels."}, {"do": "For a small UK toy shop expanding its stockroom or a carmaker buying robotics, lower repayments make more projects profitable, lifting gross investment and shifting AD rightwards.", "why": "Earns Application and Analysis marks by linking cheaper finance to firm projects and AD."}, {"do": "If the extra spending exceeds depreciation, positive net investment enlarges the UK capital stock, shifting LRAS rightwards over time and supporting non-inflationary growth.", "why": "Develops a second analytical chain connecting net investment to long-run capacity."}, {"do": "However, investment may barely rise if animal spirits are weak in a downturn, firms already have spare capacity, or risk-averse banks restrict access to credit.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by weighing constraints that can outweigh cheaper borrowing."}, {"do": "On balance, a rate cut is unlikely to raise investment significantly on its own, because firms invest mainly when they expect demand to grow; the effect will be larger if confidence and demand are already recovering, banks are willing to lend, and projects are largely debt-financed.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on the size of the effect and what it depends on."}], "answer": "Lower interest rates cut borrowing costs and the opportunity cost of retained profits, which can boost investment, AD and, through positive net investment, LRAS. Yet weak animal spirits, spare capacity or tight bank credit can stop firms investing even when loans are cheap. On balance the rise is unlikely to be significant on its own unless confidence and demand are recovering and banks are willing to lend."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 refer to animal spirits and spare capacity?", "o": ["To calculate the exact level of capital depreciation in the UK", "To argue that higher interest rates always raise business confidence", "To explain why firms might still delay investment despite cheaper borrowing", "To show that investment is excluded from the AD equation"], "a": 2, "f": ["Depreciation measures wear and tear on capital, not confidence or spare capacity.", "Higher interest rates raise costs and usually dampen rather than boost business confidence.", "If demand expectations are bleak or machines sit idle, cheaper loans alone rarely trigger new capital spending.", "Investment ($I$) is a core component of AD, already linked in Step 2."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a cut in UK corporation tax affects business investment.", "given": ["A cut in corporation tax lets UK firms keep a larger share of their profits.", "Higher post-tax retained profits provide cheaper internal finance and raise expected returns, encouraging higher investment."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["However, firms may pay extra profits as dividends or save them if demand is weak", "Therefore, every pound of tax cut becomes net investment with zero depreciation", "In addition, lower corporation tax is the same thing as a fall in Bank Rate", "Consequently, investment falls because firms only invest when tax rates are high"], "a": 0, "f": ["This questions whether firms actually spend retained profits on capital when confidence or demand is low.", "Firms rarely invest all of a tax saving, and depreciation still reduces gross to net investment.", "Corporation tax is fiscal policy set by HM Treasury, whereas Bank Rate is monetary policy.", "Higher taxes reduce post-tax returns and retained funds, discouraging rather than encouraging investment."]}, "trap": "Students often call buying shares investment, or assume lower interest rates guarantee more capital spending. Stick to physical capital goods and always evaluate confidence and credit.", "recap": "Only positive net investment expands the capital stock, and business spending depends on confidence, growth and credit as much as interest rates."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Paper 2 regularly asks you to explain how the UK trade cycle and fiscal policy alter government spending, AD and the budget balance.", "hook": {"q": "The UK enters a recession, and the Chancellor makes no new tax or spending announcements. What happens automatically to welfare outlays and tax receipts?", "o": ["Welfare outlays fall and tax receipts rise", "Both welfare outlays and tax receipts stay unchanged", "Both welfare outlays and tax receipts rise together", "Welfare outlays rise and tax receipts fall"], "a": 3, "f": ["That pattern occurs during an economic boom, not during a recession.", "Even without new policies, existing benefit rules and tax bands react automatically to GDP.", "Falling incomes and retail sales reduce income tax and VAT receipts during a downturn.", "More job losses raise benefit claims while lower incomes and spending cut tax receipts automatically."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "What counts as G", "b": "**Government expenditure** ($G$) covers current spending on public services (such as NHS staff wages) and **capital spending** on infrastructure (such as schools and hospitals). **Transfer payments** (pensions, welfare benefits) are excluded from $G$ because no good or service is produced in exchange."}, {"h": "The trade cycle", "b": "As real GDP fluctuates across the **trade cycle**, **automatic stabilisers** cushion the economy without new laws. In a recession, welfare spending rises and tax receipts fall, widening the **fiscal deficit** and limiting the fall in AD."}, {"h": "Discretionary fiscal policy", "b": "Governments also use deliberate **fiscal policy** in the Budget. **Expansionary fiscal policy** raises $G$ or cuts taxes to shift AD rightwards and reduce cyclical unemployment. **Contractionary fiscal policy** cuts $G$ or raises taxes to shift AD leftwards and curb inflation or borrowing."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how the UK trade cycle automatically affects government spending and the fiscal balance during an economic boom.", "steps": [{"do": "Define the trade cycle as fluctuations in real GDP around trend growth, and automatic stabilisers as built-in tax and spending changes that operate without new legislation.", "why": "Clear definitions distinguish automatic trade-cycle effects from discretionary policy."}, {"do": "During a boom, firms hire more staff and unemployment falls, so fewer households claim unemployment-related benefits and total welfare spending drops automatically.", "why": "Links higher employment directly to lower government transfer outlays."}, {"do": "At the same time, higher wages, company profits and retail sales automatically boost receipts from income tax, corporation tax and VAT.", "why": "Shows the revenue side of automatic stabilisers during rapid growth."}, {"do": "Lower benefit outlays and higher tax revenues shrink the fiscal deficit (or create a surplus), dampening AD growth and helping to contain inflation.", "why": "Connects spending and tax changes back to the fiscal balance and stabilisation."}], "answer": "In a boom, automatic stabilisers reduce welfare spending as unemployment falls and raise income tax, corporation tax and VAT receipts as incomes and spending grow. Together these shrink the fiscal deficit (or build a surplus) and cool AD."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 1 specify 'without new legislation' when defining automatic stabilisers?", "o": ["Because Parliament is banned from changing taxes during a boom", "To separate built-in trade-cycle effects from discretionary fiscal policy", "Because automatic stabilisers only apply to private firms", "To show that transfer payments are included inside $G$"], "a": 1, "f": ["Governments can still change taxes in a boom; that would simply be discretionary fiscal policy.", "Automatic stabilisers work under existing rules, whereas discretionary policy requires deliberate new decisions.", "Automatic stabilisers are features of the government's own tax and welfare system.", "Transfer payments are still excluded from G in AD even when they act as automatic stabilisers."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain how discretionary expansionary fiscal policy during a UK recession affects aggregate demand and the fiscal balance.", "given": ["In a recession, the government deliberately increases capital spending on new NHS hospitals and school buildings.", "Because capital spending is part of $G$, total government expenditure rises at every price level."], "q": "Which next linked point completes the explanation accurately?", "o": ["AD shifts leftwards because higher public spending instantly removes private jobs", "There is a movement down along AD and the fiscal balance moves into surplus", "AD shifts rightwards, boosting output and jobs, though the fiscal deficit widens initially", "The policy counts as an automatic stabiliser so the budget balance is unaffected"], "a": 2, "f": ["Higher G is an injection that shifts AD rightwards rather than leftwards.", "A deliberate rise in G shifts the AD curve rather than moving along it, and increases borrowing.", "Higher G shifts AD rightwards to support real output, while extra borrowing widens the deficit initially.", "Announcing new hospital construction is discretionary fiscal policy and directly raises public spending."]}, "trap": "Students often count welfare benefits inside $G$ or mix up fiscal deficits (annual flow) with national debt (total stock). Check definitions before analysing.", "recap": "Government spending reacts automatically across the trade cycle via stabilisers and deliberately through discretionary fiscal policy."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.2.5", "kind": "writing", "why": "Edexcel Paper 2 regularly asks you to evaluate how exchange rate shifts, global slowdowns or tariffs affect UK net trade and AD.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Incomes and the world economy", "b": "**Net trade** is export revenue minus import spending $(X-M)$. Faster UK real income growth lifts imports if the **marginal propensity to import** is high, worsening $(X-M)$. Strong growth in the **world economy** boosts overseas demand for UK exports."}, {"h": "Exchange rates and SPICED", "b": "The **exchange rate** is the price of one currency in terms of another. An **appreciation** (stronger pound) makes imports cheaper and exports dearer (SPICED), tending to worsen $(X-M)$. A depreciation makes UK exports cheaper abroad and imports pricier."}, {"h": "Protectionism and non-price factors", "b": "**Protectionism** uses **tariffs** or quotas to restrict imports and shield domestic firms, though retaliation can cut UK exports. **Non-price factors** such as quality, design, reliability and marketing also shape export demand."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the likely effect of a 10% depreciation of the pound (£) on UK net trade $(X-M)$.", "steps": [{"do": "A depreciation is a fall in sterling's value against other currencies, making UK exports cheaper in foreign currency and imports dearer in pounds (the reverse of SPICED).", "why": "Earns Knowledge marks by defining depreciation and stating both price effects accurately."}, {"do": "A 10% fall makes a UK export about 10% cheaper in foreign currency and a foreign good about 11% dearer in pounds, so overseas orders rise ($X$ up) and UK buyers switch to domestic rivals (import volumes down).", "why": "Earns Application marks by using the 10% figure, and Analysis marks by tracing how relative prices alter export and import volumes."}, {"do": "If volumes respond enough, export revenue rises and import spending falls, so net trade $(X-M)$ improves, shifting the UK AD curve to the right.", "why": "Links the trade response directly to the AD diagram for full analysis."}, {"do": "However, in the short run, existing contracts and scarce UK substitutes make $X$ and $M$ price-inelastic, so higher import bills can worsen $(X-M)$ before volumes adjust later.", "why": "Earns Evaluation marks by applying price elasticity of demand and short-run time lags."}, {"do": "On balance, net trade is likely to worsen briefly and then improve (the J-curve), because demand becomes more price-elastic as contracts end; the gain needs export and import price elasticities together to exceed one (the Marshall-Lerner condition), and its size depends on UK non-price competitiveness and how much imported content UK exports contain.", "why": "Earns top-level Evaluation by reaching a supported judgement on timing and on what the outcome depends on."}], "answer": "A 10% depreciation makes UK exports about 10% cheaper abroad and imports about 11% dearer in pounds, tending to raise $X$, cut $M$ and shift AD rightwards. In the short run, inelastic demand can make $(X-M)$ worse first. On balance net trade is likely to improve after a lag, provided demand becomes elastic enough, by an amount that depends on non-price competitiveness and imported inputs."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does Step 4 argue that net trade can worsen in the short run after a depreciation?", "o": ["Inelastic demand means higher import prices raise import bills before volumes adjust", "A weaker pound makes UK exports more expensive in foreign currency", "Tariffs automatically rise whenever a currency depreciates", "Foreign incomes always fall whenever sterling weakens"], "a": 0, "f": ["When buyers cannot switch quickly, paying more pounds per import lifts $M$ expenditure while $X$ barely rises.", "A weaker pound makes UK exports cheaper, not dearer, in foreign currency.", "Exchange rate shifts do not automatically trigger government tariffs.", "A fall in sterling does not automatically reduce real incomes in overseas economies."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate how a sharp recession in the Eurozone affects UK net trade $(X-M)$.", "given": ["The Eurozone is a major export market for UK goods and services.", "Falling European real incomes reduce overseas demand for UK exports, worsening $(X-M)$ and shifting UK AD leftwards."], "q": "Which next sentence earns strong Edexcel evaluation marks?", "o": ["Therefore, UK imports from the Eurozone immediately fall to zero at the same time", "In addition, a Eurozone recession is defined as an appreciation of the UK pound", "Consequently, UK net trade improves because lower exports increase $(X-M)$", "However, the hit shrinks if UK firms sell income-inelastic goods or diversify markets"], "a": 3, "f": ["UK imports depend on UK real incomes and prices, and never fall to zero.", "A Eurozone recession is a fall in European real GDP, not an exchange rate definition.", "Lower exports reduce rather than improve net trade $(X-M)$.", "This weighs income elasticity and market diversification to judge how severe the export fall will be."]}, "trap": "Students often assume a weaker pound guarantees a trade surplus or ignore retaliation against tariffs. Always check price elasticity of demand, time lags and non-price quality.", "recap": "Net trade responds to UK and world incomes, sterling's exchange rate, tariffs and non-price quality, but elasticity and lags shape the outcome."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.4.4", "kind": "method", "why": "Multiplier calculations appear in the multiple-choice and short-answer sections, and the multiplier also supports AD and fiscal policy evaluation in longer answers.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "What the multiplier does", "b": "An **injection** (investment, government spending or exports) raises incomes. Some of that income is re-spent on UK goods, which creates more income for others. The final rise in **national income** is larger than the first injection. This is the **multiplier effect**."}, {"h": "Calculating the multiplier", "b": "The **marginal propensity to withdraw** (MPW) is MPS + MPT + MPM. The **multiplier** is $k=\\frac{1}{MPW}$. If all of MPC is spent on UK output, this equals $\\frac{1}{1-MPC}$. Then: change in income = $k$ × injection."}, {"h": "Effect on AD", "b": "The initial injection shifts **aggregate demand** right. The multiplier makes the final shift larger. A higher MPS, MPT or MPM makes the multiplier **smaller**, because more of each extra £ leaves the circular flow."}], "example": {"task": "MPS = 0.2, MPT = 0.1, MPM = 0.1. Firms raise investment by £4bn. Calculate the total change in national income.", "steps": [{"do": "Add the withdrawals: MPW = 0.2 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.4.", "why": "Saving, tax and imports all leave the circular flow of income."}, {"do": "Find the multiplier: $k=\\frac{1}{0.4}=2.5$.", "why": "The multiplier is 1 divided by the MPW."}, {"do": "Multiply: change in income = 2.5 × £4bn = £10bn.", "why": "The multiplier scales the initial injection to the final change."}, {"do": "Check: MPC on UK goods = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6, and $\\frac{1}{1-0.6}=2.5$.", "why": "Both formulas must agree, which confirms the working."}], "answer": "National income rises by £10bn (k = 2.5, from MPW = 0.4, applied to the £4bn investment injection)."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example add MPS, MPT and MPM before finding the multiplier?", "o": ["Each is a share of extra income that is not re-spent on UK output", "Each is a share of extra income that is spent on UK goods", "The three must always add up to 1 for the formula to work", "Only the largest withdrawal affects the size of the multiplier"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. All three are withdrawals from the circular flow, so together they set how much is passed on.", "Wrong: these are the leakages. The share spent on UK goods is what remains after withdrawals.", "Wrong: they sum to MPW, which is below 1. Only MPW plus domestic MPC equals 1.", "Wrong: every withdrawal matters. Using only the biggest one overstates the multiplier."]}, "faded": {"task": "MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.1 and MPM = 0.05. Government spending rises by £3bn. Calculate the total change in national income.", "given": ["MPW = 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.05 = 0.25"], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["k = 0.25 × 3 = 0.75", "k = 1/0.75 ≈ 1.33", "k = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75", "k = 1/0.25 = 4"], "a": 3, "f": ["Wrong: this multiplies MPW by the injection. The multiplier is 1 divided by MPW, and you apply it afterwards.", "Wrong: 0.75 is the share not withdrawn. Dividing 1 by it gives the wrong multiplier.", "Wrong: 1 − MPW is not the multiplier. You must take 1 divided by MPW.", "Right. k = 1/MPW = 4. Then multiply by £3bn to get a £12bn rise in national income."]}, "trap": "Leaving out one withdrawal, usually MPM or MPT, or stopping at k. Add all three withdrawals, invert the total, then multiply by the injection.", "recap": "Multiplier = 1/(MPS + MPT + MPM); multiply it by the injection to find the total change in national income."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "2.6.2", "kind": "writing", "why": "Demand-side policy appears in extended evaluation questions, where you must analyse a tool and judge how effective it is in a given context.", "hook": null, "chunks": [{"h": "Monetary policy tools", "b": "The **Monetary Policy Committee** at the Bank of England sets **Bank Rate** to meet the CPI inflation target of 2%. It can also use **quantitative easing**, creating money to buy bonds, which lowers long-term yields and boosts liquidity."}, {"h": "Fiscal policy tools", "b": "**Fiscal policy** changes **government spending** and **taxation**. Spending more or taxing less is expansionary and widens the **budget deficit**. The reverse is contractionary. Annual deficits add to the **national debt**, the accumulated stock."}, {"h": "Transmission to AD", "b": "Lower Bank Rate cuts borrowing costs, raising consumption (C) and investment (I), and weakens the pound, helping net exports. Tax cuts raise **disposable income**. Spending raises G directly. AD shifts right, with a **multiplier** effect."}, {"h": "Evaluating effectiveness", "b": "Judge by: **time lags**, **confidence**, **spare capacity** (output gap), size of the multiplier, **crowding out**, and debt levels. After the 2008 crisis, rates near zero and QE had limited effect where banks restricted lending, so effectiveness varies."}], "example": {"task": "Evaluate the use of quantitative easing to raise aggregate demand in a recession. (Model answer built in parts.)", "steps": [{"do": "QE is when the Bank of England creates electronic money to buy government bonds from financial institutions, which raises bond prices and lowers yields.", "why": "Defines the tool accurately and shows knowledge of how it works."}, {"do": "Lower yields cut long-term borrowing costs and extra liquidity may increase lending, so investment and consumption rise, shifting AD to the right.", "why": "A clear chain of analysis linking QE to AD."}, {"do": "In a deep recession with a large negative output gap, higher AD would raise real GDP and reduce unemployment without much inflation.", "why": "Applies analysis to the context given."}, {"do": "However, if confidence is low or banks hoard the cash, spending may barely rise, and asset price rises can widen wealth inequality.", "why": "Evaluation: limits of the policy, with reasoning."}, {"do": "Overall, QE helps most when combined with fiscal support and when banks are willing to lend, so its success depends on confidence.", "why": "A judgement supported by a condition."}], "answer": "QE lowers yields and adds liquidity, raising AD, but works only if confidence and lending respond, so it is most effective alongside fiscal support."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer mention the output gap when judging QE?", "o": ["It shows spare capacity, so higher AD raises output rather than just prices", "It proves QE will definitely work", "It shows the government's budget is balanced", "It shows inflation must be above target"], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["With spare capacity, extra demand mostly lifts real GDP, so the policy is more likely to be appropriate.", "Context only makes success more likely, never certain; claiming certainty is poor evaluation.", "The output gap measures actual versus potential output, not the budget position.", "A negative gap usually goes with weak, not high, inflation."]}, "faded": {"task": "Evaluate the use of a cut in income tax to reduce unemployment.", "given": ["Define fiscal policy: tax cuts raise disposable income.", "Analysis: higher consumption raises AD, so firms hire more workers."], "q": "Which is the best next sentence, offering evaluation?", "o": ["The effect depends on the marginal propensity to consume, since savers or high earners spend less of the extra income.", "Income tax is a tax on income paid by workers.", "Unemployment is when people want work but cannot find it.", "Therefore, unemployment will certainly fall."], "a": 0, "x": "", "f": ["It gives a reasoned limitation tied to the multiplier, which earns evaluation marks.", "This repeats a definition and adds no evaluation.", "This is a definition of unemployment, adding no judgement.", "Asserting certainty with no justification is unsupported and not evaluation."]}, "trap": "Listing tools without judging them. Always add a limit (lags, confidence, spare capacity, debt) and a conclusion that depends on context.", "recap": "Demand-side policy shifts AD through Bank Rate, QE, taxes and spending, but its success depends on context and confidence."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Profit maximisation, types of profit and the shutdown decision appear in short-answer and data-response questions, and underpin analysis of firms' behaviour in every market structure.", "hook": {"q": "A toy shop's revenue just covers all its costs, including the wage the owner could earn working elsewhere. Will the owner probably keep the shop open in the long run?", "o": ["Yes, because the owner earns as much as the next best use of their time", "No, because there is no profit left over at all", "Only if the price of toys rises", "No, because a firm needs supernormal profit to stay open"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Opportunity cost is covered, so staying is as good as the next best option. This is normal profit.", "Wrong. Costs include the owner's opportunity cost, so there is a profit that is just enough to stay.", "Wrong. A price rise is not needed; the current position already keeps the owner in the market.", "Wrong. Supernormal profit is above the minimum; normal profit alone is enough to stay."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Profit maximising output", "b": "A firm maximises profit where **MC = MR**, with MC cutting MR from below. If MR > MC, an extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so output rises. If MC > MR, an extra unit reduces profit, so output falls."}, {"h": "Normal and supernormal profit", "b": "**Normal profit** is the minimum profit needed to keep a firm in its current line of production. It covers opportunity cost, and occurs where AR = AC. **Supernormal profit** is profit above normal, where AR > AC. Its area is (AR − AC) × Q."}, {"h": "Losses", "b": "A firm makes a **loss** where AR < AC at its output. The loss is shown by the area (AC − AR) × Q. Total revenue is below total cost, so the firm earns less than normal profit."}, {"h": "The shutdown rule", "b": "In the **short run**, a firm shuts down if AR < **AVC**, because it cannot cover even its variable costs. If AR ≥ AVC it keeps producing and contributes towards fixed costs. In the **long run**, it exits if AR < AC."}], "example": {"task": "At 100 units a firm has AR £10, AVC £8 and AC £12. Explain why it may continue producing in the short run.", "steps": [{"do": "AR (£10) is below AC (£12), so the firm makes a loss of (£12 − £10) × 100 = £200.", "why": "Identifies the loss using the area (AC − AR) × Q."}, {"do": "AR (£10) is above AVC (£8), so revenue covers all variable costs.", "why": "The short-run shutdown test compares AR with AVC."}, {"do": "Each unit adds £2 above its variable cost, a total contribution of £200 towards fixed costs.", "why": "Shows that producing still helps pay fixed costs."}, {"do": "Fixed costs are (£12 − £8) × 100 = £400 and are paid even at zero output. Closing loses £400; producing loses £200.", "why": "Compares the two losses to show producing is better."}, {"do": "So it continues in the short run. If AR stays below AC in the long run, it should exit.", "why": "Conclusion, distinguishing short run from long run."}], "answer": "The firm makes a £200 loss as AR is below AC. AR exceeds AVC, so it earns £200 towards fixed costs of £400. Closing would lose £400, so it continues in the short run, but exits in the long run if AR stays below AC."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example compare AR with AVC rather than AC to decide on short-run shutdown?", "o": ["Fixed costs must be paid even if output is zero, so only variable costs can be avoided", "AVC is always higher than AC, so it is the stricter test", "Profit is maximised where AR = AVC", "Fixed costs do not count as costs in the short run"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Fixed costs are unavoidable in the short run, so the choice depends on whether revenue covers avoidable variable costs.", "Wrong. AVC is always below AC, because AC includes fixed cost per unit.", "Wrong. Profit is maximised where MC = MR; AR = AVC is only the shutdown point.", "Wrong. Fixed costs are real costs; they are just unavoidable in the short run."]}, "faded": {"task": "At 50 units a firm has AR £5, AVC £6 and AC £9. Should it continue in the short run?", "given": ["Loss per unit is £9 − £5 = £4, so the total loss is £4 × 50 = £200.", "AR (£5) is below AVC (£6), so revenue does not cover variable costs."], "q": "What is the next step?", "o": ["Continue; revenue of £250 is positive, so some fixed costs are covered", "Shut down; producing loses £200 but closing loses only the £150 fixed costs", "Continue; the £200 loss is smaller than the £450 total cost", "Shut down only in the long run, because AR is below AC rather than AVC"], "a": 1, "f": ["Wrong. Revenue of £250 is less than variable costs of £300, so it covers none of the fixed costs.", "Right. Fixed costs are (£9 − £6) × 50 = £150. Producing loses £200, closing loses £150, so shut down.", "Wrong. Comparing the loss with total cost is meaningless. The comparison is with fixed costs lost on closing.", "Wrong. AR below AVC is already the short-run shutdown condition."]}, "trap": "Shutting down whenever AR < AC. In the short run, compare AR with AVC. Only if AR < AVC should the firm close; the long-run test is AR < AC.", "recap": "Maximise profit where MC = MR; shut down in the short run if AR < AVC, and leave in the long run if AR < AC."}
{"s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "kind": "idea", "why": "Oligopoly appears in short-answer and extended questions, often asking you to explain collusion, game theory or non-price competition using a payoff matrix or market-share data.", "hook": {"q": "Two petrol stations face each other on one road. One cuts its price by 3p. What is the most likely response from the other?", "o": ["It ignores the cut because its own price is unaffected", "It matches or beats the cut to avoid losing customers", "It raises its price to show it is higher quality", "It leaves the market at once"], "a": 1, "f": ["Ignoring the cut would lose it customers, so a rival with few competitors will usually respond.", "Right. With few rivals, each firm's decision affects the other, so firms react. This is interdependence.", "Raising price after a rival cuts would lose even more customers, so this is rarely the typical reaction.", "Firms rarely exit over a small price cut; they respond with their own pricing first."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Features and concentration", "b": "An **oligopoly** has a few large firms that dominate the market, so firms are **interdependent**. Dominance is measured by the **concentration ratio**: the combined market share of the top n firms. Three-firm ratio = (sales of top three ÷ total market sales) × 100."}, {"h": "Barriers to entry", "b": "High **barriers to entry** protect oligopolists' profits. Examples include **economies of scale**, strong **branding**, high start-up costs, control of distribution and **sunk costs**. They let firms earn **supernormal profit** in the long run, because new firms cannot easily enter."}, {"h": "Collusion", "b": "**Collusion** means firms act together to limit competition, for example by fixing prices or output. It can be formal (a **cartel**) or tacit. It raises joint profit, but each member can gain by **cheating**, so it is unstable. Collusion is generally illegal in the UK."}, {"h": "Game theory and competition", "b": "**Game theory** studies strategic choices between interdependent firms. In a **prisoners' dilemma**, each firm's **dominant strategy** leads both to a worse outcome than cooperation. **Price competition** invites matching and price wars. **Non-price competition** uses branding, quality and service."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why firms in an oligopoly may prefer non-price competition to price competition.", "steps": [{"do": "State that oligopolies have few large firms, so each is interdependent with its rivals.", "why": "This links the answer to the key feature of the market."}, {"do": "Explain that a price cut is easily seen and quickly matched by rivals.", "why": "Shows the chain: reaction by rivals means no lasting gain in market share."}, {"do": "Explain that if everyone cuts prices, a price war follows and revenue and profit per unit fall for all firms.", "why": "Develops the consequence of price competition."}, {"do": "Explain that branding, advertising and quality are harder to copy quickly, so they build loyalty and protect margins.", "why": "Links non-price competition to the benefit, completing the contrast."}], "answer": "Few large, interdependent firms notice and match price cuts, so no share is gained and a price war lowers profits. Non-price competition, such as branding or quality, is harder to copy quickly, builds loyalty and protects margins."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the answer say rivals are likely to match a price cut?", "o": ["Because the products are identical and consumers have perfect information", "Because barriers to entry stop any rival from setting its own price", "Because there are few firms, so each notices and reacts to the others' decisions", "Because demand for each firm's product is perfectly elastic"], "a": 2, "f": ["Identical products with perfect information describe perfect competition, not oligopoly.", "Barriers to entry affect new firms, not how existing rivals respond to a price cut.", "Right. With few firms, interdependence means each firm sees and responds to rivals' price changes.", "Perfectly elastic demand is a perfect competition feature; oligopoly firms face downward-sloping demand."]}, "faded": {"task": "Two firms each choose Advertise or Not. Profits (£m, A then B): neither 8, 8; both 5, 5; only A advertises 11, 3; only B advertises 3, 11. Explain why both may advertise.", "given": ["If B does not advertise, A earns £11m by advertising instead of £8m.", "If B advertises, A earns £5m by advertising instead of £3m."], "q": "What is the next step in the explanation?", "o": ["A should not advertise, as both firms would then earn £8m, which is a stable outcome", "Advertising is A's dominant strategy, and the same is true for B, so both advertise and earn £5m each", "The firms will collude, so both advertise and each earns £11m", "A advertises only if B does not, so the outcome is uncertain"], "a": 1, "f": ["Not advertising is best jointly, but it is not stable because each firm gains by deviating.", "Right. Advertising is better whatever the rival does, so both do it and end up with £5m, worse than £8m.", "£11m is earned only when one firm advertises alone; both advertising gives £5m each.", "A earns more by advertising in both cases, so the choice is not uncertain; it is a dominant strategy."]}, "trap": "Treating the concentration ratio as an average or dividing by the wrong total. Always add the top n sales, divide by total market sales, then multiply by 100.", "recap": "Few interdependent firms, high barriers and rivals' reactions explain collusion's instability, game-theory outcomes and why non-price competition is often preferred."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.1.8", "kind": "idea", "why": "Exchange rate systems and the J-curve appear in short-answer and data-response questions, and support evaluation in essays on policy and the current account.", "hook": {"q": "A country's currency falls in value. Imports now cost more in that currency. In the first few weeks, what do you expect to happen to the quantity of imports bought?", "o": ["It barely changes, because people cannot switch quickly", "It falls sharply at once", "It rises, because imports are in the news", "It falls to zero"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Contracts and habits delay adjustment, which is the idea behind the J-curve.", "Quick falls need elastic demand, and short-run demand is usually inelastic.", "There is no reason for quantity to rise when prices go up.", "Demand rarely drops to zero; some imports are essential."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Three exchange rate systems", "b": "A **floating** rate is set by market demand and supply. A **fixed** rate is held at a target by the central bank. A **managed** rate floats but the central bank intervenes, for example to keep it within a band."}, {"h": "Changes in the rate", "b": "Under floating rates a market-driven fall is **depreciation** and a rise is **appreciation**. Under fixed rates a deliberate cut is **devaluation** and a deliberate rise is **revaluation**. The government decides, not the market."}, {"h": "Defending a fixed rate", "b": "To stop the rate falling, the central bank buys its own currency using **foreign reserves** and raises **interest rates** to attract inflows. Reserves are limited, so this cannot last forever."}, {"h": "The J-curve", "b": "After a fall in the rate, the trade balance often worsens first because demand is **inelastic** in the short run. Later, volumes adjust and the balance improves if the **Marshall-Lerner condition** holds: the sum of PED for exports and PED for imports exceeds 1."}], "example": {"task": "Explain why a devaluation of the pound, if it were pegged, might worsen the current account before improving it.", "steps": [{"do": "Define devaluation: a deliberate cut in the pegged rate, so each pound buys less foreign currency.", "why": "Shows the correct term and the starting point of the chain."}, {"do": "Import prices in pounds rise; export prices in foreign currency fall.", "why": "Links the rate change to relative prices."}, {"do": "In the short run demand for both is inelastic, so volumes change little.", "why": "Explains why contracts and habits delay a response."}, {"do": "Import spending rises and export earnings barely change, so the deficit widens.", "why": "Completes the downward part of the J-curve."}, {"do": "Over time volumes adjust; if the Marshall-Lerner condition is met, the balance improves.", "why": "Shows the recovery and the condition needed."}], "answer": "Devaluation raises import prices and lowers export prices, but short-run inelastic demand means volumes change little, so the deficit widens first. Later volumes adjust and, if the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, the balance improves."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the example say import spending rises in the short run after devaluation?", "o": ["Import prices rise in pounds while quantities hardly fall", "Import quantities rise because they are cheaper", "Exports fall, which raises import spending", "The central bank buys more imports"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Higher price times almost unchanged quantity means higher spending.", "Imports become dearer after devaluation, not cheaper.", "Exports do not directly set import spending; the cause is price and quantity of imports.", "The central bank does not buy imports; households and firms do."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why raising interest rates helps a central bank defend a fixed exchange rate that is under downward pressure.", "given": ["Downward pressure means supply of the currency exceeds demand at the target rate.", "Higher interest rates make UK assets more attractive to foreign savers."], "q": "What is the next step in the chain?", "o": ["Foreigners buy the currency to hold these assets, raising demand and supporting the rate", "Foreigners sell the currency, raising supply and supporting the rate", "Exports become cheaper, raising demand at once", "The central bank sells reserves to cut demand"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right. Hot money inflows raise demand for the currency, offsetting the downward pressure.", "Selling the currency would push the rate down further, not support it.", "Interest rates work via capital flows first; export prices are not directly changed.", "Selling reserves to cut demand reverses the logic; it needs buying the currency."]}, "trap": "Do not call a deliberate fall in a fixed rate depreciation, and do not assume a weaker currency improves the current account at once. Check the system and the elasticities.", "recap": "Floating rates move with the market; fixed rates are held by intervention; after a fall, the J-curve means the trade balance worsens before it improves."}
{"s": "macro", "t": "4.2.2", "kind": "idea", "why": "Inequality questions in Papers 1 and 3 ask you to explain, interpret or evaluate measures like the Lorenz curve and Gini, and give causes of inequality.", "hook": {"q": "In an economy, the poorest 50% of households receive exactly 50% of total income, the poorest 20% receive 20%, and so on. What would its Lorenz curve look like?", "o": ["A straight 45° line from the origin", "A curve bowed far below the 45° line", "A horizontal line along the bottom", "A vertical line up the right-hand side"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: every x% of households gets x% of income, which is perfect equality.", "Bowing below the line would mean the poor get less than their share of income.", "A flat line would mean most households get nothing, which is extreme inequality.", "A vertical line is not a possible Lorenz curve shape here; the axes are cumulative shares."]}, "chunks": [{"h": "Income and wealth", "b": "**Income** is a flow of earnings over a period, such as wages, rent, interest and benefits. **Wealth** is a stock of assets at a point in time, such as property, shares and savings. Wealth is usually distributed more unequally than income."}, {"h": "The Lorenz curve", "b": "A **Lorenz curve** plots the cumulative % of income (vertical) against the cumulative % of households (horizontal), poorest first. The **line of equality** is the 45° line. The further the curve bows below it, the greater the inequality."}, {"h": "The Gini coefficient", "b": "The **Gini coefficient** is $\\frac{A}{A+B}$, where A is the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve, and B is the area beneath the curve. It runs from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality)."}, {"h": "Causes of inequality", "b": "Causes include differences in **skills and education**, wage differentials from labour market demand and supply, unequal **ownership of assets** that earn returns, discrimination, unemployment, inheritance and **government policy** on tax and benefits."}], "example": {"task": "Explain how a Lorenz curve shows the extent of income inequality in an economy.", "steps": [{"do": "A Lorenz curve plots the cumulative % of income against the cumulative % of households, ranked from poorest to richest.", "why": "Defines the axes first, which shows accurate knowledge."}, {"do": "If income were shared equally, the curve would follow the 45° line of equality, since x% of households would receive x% of income.", "why": "Gives the benchmark that the curve is compared against."}, {"do": "In reality the curve bows below the line, because the poorest households get a smaller share of income than their share of households.", "why": "Links the shape of the curve to unequal outcomes."}, {"do": "The bigger the gap between the curve and the line, the greater the inequality, and the higher the Gini coefficient.", "why": "Connects the diagram to the Gini and the conclusion."}], "answer": "The Lorenz curve plots cumulative income against cumulative households, poorest first. Equality is the 45° line. The further the curve bows below it, the more unequal income is, and the larger the area between them, so the higher the Gini."}, "selfx": {"q": "Why does the 45° line represent perfect equality on a Lorenz diagram?", "o": ["Because at every point, x% of households receive x% of income", "Because the poorest households receive no income on it", "Because the Gini coefficient on that line equals 1", "Because the line shows average income for each household"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: equal shares mean cumulative income share always equals cumulative household share.", "On the line the poorest get a fair share, not zero; zero for the poor would be extreme inequality.", "The Gini on the line is 0, because there is no area between it and the curve.", "The axes show cumulative percentages, not average income levels."]}, "faded": {"task": "Explain why wealth tends to be distributed more unequally than income.", "given": ["Wealth is a stock of assets, such as property and shares, built up over time.", "Some households inherit assets, while others start with none."], "q": "Which is the best next linked point?", "o": ["Assets earn returns such as rent and dividends, which can be saved, so wealth grows cumulatively", "Wealth is a flow, so it changes more quickly than income each month", "A high Gini for wealth proves that income is equally shared", "Everyone must pay wealth tax, which keeps holdings equal"], "a": 0, "f": ["Right: owning assets produces income that can be saved into more assets, widening the gap over time.", "Wrong: wealth is a stock, not a flow. This is the stock/flow slip.", "Wrong: a high Gini means high inequality, and it says nothing to prove equal income.", "Wrong: there is no universal wealth tax in the UK that equalises holdings; this is unsupported."]}, "trap": "Reading a higher Gini as more equal, or treating wealth as a flow. Always say: higher Gini means more unequal, and wealth is a stock of assets.", "recap": "Lorenz curve further from the 45° line means a higher Gini and more inequality; wealth is a stock, income a flow."}
